#align(center, box(width: 90%, align(left)[
  *Summary.* The claim this plan serves is that a language like Ochr makes a good general-purpose language. That needs two guarantees, _consistency_, so that its proofs can be trusted, and _type safety_, so that accepted programs never crash or go wrong when run. The plan proves both by _meaning preservation_. Types are given meanings in a set model, in which `False` denotes the empty set. Every state the checker reaches while checking a definition is given a meaning too, as a function of what its unknowns stand for: an _assignment_ gives each unknown any element of its type's meaning, non-computable functions included. The central theorem has three parts, proved together by one ordinary induction: each step of the checker is matched by the meaning of the term it runs, under every assignment; every value the checker stores lies in the meaning of its type, whatever the borrows still live may later write; and the state stays well formed. An accepted definition therefore means an element of its type, and a closed proof of `False` would be an element of the empty set. All four planned paper tests have passed (@sec-tests): the set model exists on a slice chosen to contain everything that made its definition doubtful; states with live borrows have a meaning, as functions of their borrows' final contents, and the step lemmas hold on a higher-order slice, much of both checked in Lean; each of the four closed proofs of `False` the checker once accepted and has since fixed is a step at which one of the theorem's three parts fails; and an audit of every decision the checker makes left one item to settle. The one failure found is a fifth closed proof of `False`, in which the generic call and an instance disagreed on whether a place is moved. The checker rules it out by dropping at the join: a match moves, on every path, what any of its arms moves, so a moved place means _moved_ everywhere, and equations decide on it. Type safety reuses the same meanings: a closed run of an accepted function means what the reference run of its call says, and that reference run never goes wrong; test 3 checked it on a slice, with no checker bug found: errors at runtime reflect to the reference run or, at one-element types, to the checker's derivation; `J` at runtime is its transported term; and erasure holds by construction. What remains for it is chiefly a realizability relation for calls through function values (@sec-safety). A Lean development of the central theorem against the real checker is under way (@sec-spine): its four core lemmas are proved, every step lemma is stated and none is proved yet, and stating them changed the form of the theorem's first part, from an order between meanings to a simulation by the reference run (@def-sim), whose relation has since been found wrong four more times, and repaired each time. It has found faults in the model's definitions and in the statements, and one checker fault, with three relatives, in when a borrow ends: the checker now decides that the same way on every path (D90), as Rust does, and the reference run reads it the same static way. Until a redesign of how the reference run types a call's result lands, the mechanised step lemmas are false on some accepted programs, among them a parameter whose function type is computed by a call (@def-refrun). What remains is listed in @sec-order.
]))
