= What this document is <sec-intro>

This is the plan we intend to follow to prove that Ochr is consistent and type safe: the shape of the argument, the definitions it needs, the lemmas it reduces to, which of those are already proved and where, and where the plan could fail. It is written as a section of theory work would be, so the statements are precise where they can be, and where they cannot be yet, that is said.

*The object of study.* The calculus is the one implemented by the Lean checker in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/` (chiefly `Syntax.lean`, `Basic.lean`, `Env.lean`, `Machine.lean`, `Decide.lean`, `Obs.lean`, `Check.lean` and `Run.lean`) and laid out rule by rule in the appendix of the Ochr paper. @sec-prelim restates every part of it that the plan relies on, so that the plan can be followed without either.

*Status labels.* Each lemma carries one of four labels.
- #s-lean: proved in Lean against the real checker's types, with no `sorry`, on Lean's three standard axioms.
- #s-red[X]: shown to follow from the named lemmas X by an argument given here.
- #s-paper: an argument exists at the level of detail of this document, not checked in Lean.
- #s-open: no argument yet.
Design questions that must be decided before an argument can be written are marked #s-design.

*Route.* The plan follows _meaning preservation_. Every type, value and checker state is given a mathematical meaning (a _denotational model_), and each step the checker takes is shown to keep the meaning of its state. Truth is defined in the model, so a step that keeps meaning cannot lead the checker from true premises to a false conclusion. This is the usual soundness argument for _symbolic execution_ (@sec-route), with set-theoretic meanings in place of concrete machine states. No second, concrete run is involved: the checker's run is compared with what it means, by a relation between the checker's state and a state of meanings (@def-sim). Another route, translating each accepted program into Lean and having Lean's kernel check it, gives consistency one program at a time; it is not part of this plan, though its results are used in @sec-risks as evidence.

*Mechanisation.* A Lean development of the central theorem (@thm-mp) against the checker's own types is under way on the branch `spine`, in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/Meaning/`. Its design note, `ochr/docs/31-meaning-spine.md` on that branch, is the detailed record of every definition, statement and counterexample; @sec-spine says where it stands. Where it has corrected this plan, the plan below is stated in the corrected form.

= The calculus, as far as the plan needs it <sec-prelim>

== Terms, values and neutrals

Terms and types share one grammar: variables, the universes `Prop` and $ty("Type")_i$, n-ary Π-types, recursive functions $kw("fix") f (overline(x) : overline(A)) : B kw("by") x_j := t$ (a `fix` without `by` is a λ), saturated calls, inductive types $ty("D")(overline(a))$ and constructors $ty("C")(overline(t))$, the primitive equality $ty("Eq") A space t space u$, transport $ty("J")(A, a, b, P, h, t)$ and $kw("rewrite") h kw("in") t$, borrow types $\&A$, places $p ::= x | *p | p.g$, borrowing $\&p$, assignment $p := t$, `let`, sequencing $t; u$, $kw("match") p space {ty("C")_1 (overline(y)_1) => t_1, dots}$ with any number of arms, $kw("split") f kw("in") t$, and $ty("Id") A space t space u$, the equality of two computations.

Every type of data and every connective is an inductive declaration $kw("inductive") ty("D") (overline(x) : overline(X)) : s := ty("C")_1 (overline(g)_1 : overline(B)_1) | dots$ with $s in {ty("Prop"), ty("Type")_0}$. The library declares `Nat`, `Unit`, `Pair`, `False` (no constructors), `True` (one constructor `I`, written `refl`) and `And`. A field's type may mention the type being declared, other declared types, parameters, earlier fields of the same constructor (the fields form a _telescope_), calls of earlier _type functions_ (definitions whose codomain is a sort), and equations between _first-order data_ (types built from inductive types and parameters, with no Π, no `&` and no sort); never `Π` or `&`. Parameters whose declared type is syntactically a sort are _type parameters_; the others are _value parameters_, and a recursive occurrence of $ty("D")$ in a field may take any arguments at them (_non-uniform_ occurrences). A family indexed by values is written by fixing the index with an equation field (_Fording_): `Vec(A, n) := VNil(h : Eq(Nat, n, Z)) | VCons(m : Nat, x : A, xs : Vec(A, m), h : Eq(Nat, n, S(m)))`.

$\&A$ requires $A : ty("Type")_0$, and `&` occurs only as the whole declared type of a parameter, result or annotated term: never inside another type and never as the result of computing a type. So there are no borrows inside data and no borrows of borrows. Universes are not cumulative, and `Prop : Type₁` beside `Type₀ : Type₁`.

*Values* are constructor values $ty("C")(overline(a); overline(v))$ (recording the inductive's parameters $overline(a)$), the proof value `⋆`, top-level function names, closures $chevron.l overline(kappa) tack.r kw("fix") dots chevron.r$ (code with captured values $overline(kappa)$), Π-closures $chevron.l overline(kappa) tack.r Pi(overline(x) : overline(A)). B chevron.r$ (types are values), type values, borrows $"borrow"_ell v$, loans $"loan"_ell$, the moved-out marker `⊥`, and _neutrals_. A neutral is an _abstract value_ σ (an unknown of a known type Δ(σ), introduced only by the checker) or a _sealed program_ #seal[$t$] (a program with no free variables whose run is stuck). A value is _borrow-free_ if it contains no borrow and no live loan. A value is _closed_ if it contains no free variables, no abstract values, no loans and no `⊥`: nothing whose meaning depends on an environment, on what the unknowns stand for, or on contents held elsewhere in the state. A closed value therefore means the same thing wherever it appears, which is what lets the model give it a meaning $deno(v)$ on its own (@sec-model). A sealed program has no free variables but is stuck on some abstract value, so it is not closed; nor is anything containing one.

== States, borrows and resolution <sec-states>

An environment Ω is a stack of frames, each binding variables (with declared types) to values, plus temporaries. A _state_ adds Δ (the types of abstract values), ρ (the refinements and generalisation records made so far), the goal while a definition is checked, and the recursion stack. $"content"_Omega (p)$ follows a place through variables, borrows ($*p$ looks inside $"borrow"_ell v$ at $v$) and fields.

*Borrows.* $\&p$ moves the content $v$ of $p$ into $"borrow"_ell v$ and leaves $"loan"_ell$ at $p$. _Ending_ the borrow ([End]) replaces it by `⊥` and substitutes its content for every occurrence of $"loan"_ell$: a loan is a variable bound by its borrow. Every access to a place is preceded by [Access], which ends every borrow whose loan lies on the path to the place or inside its content (for a match, which inspects only the head: on the path, at the head, or anywhere inside a neutral head). Reading non-copy data moves it out, leaving `⊥`; data of a _copy type_ (`Unit`, sorts, propositions, inductives declared `copy`, and non-recursive inductives whose fields are copy types) is copied, copy-ness being read from the place's declared type, in which a type parameter is never a copy type; and $kw("clone")(p)$ copies explicitly. The machine's rules for these steps are [Borrow], [Read] (moving data out), [Move] (moving a borrow), [Copy], [Clone] and [Assign]; [Ctor] builds a constructor value. A borrow must be whole (no `⊥` inside) when it ends, is returned, or is passed to a call that closes off.

*Dropping.* When an owned value dies holding a live loan, [Drop] ends the borrower if the borrower is held in a binding, and fails if the borrower is a value in flight (a temporary such as a result being returned). So a local may die while a variable that borrowed it is never used again, but nothing may return a borrow of its own local.

*Owners and resolution.* $"owners"_Omega (ell)$ follows each occurrence of $"loan"_ell$ outward: through the content of an enclosing $"borrow"_m$ to $"owners"_Omega (m)$, otherwise to the owned position holding it. The _resolution_ $res(Omega)$ ends every borrow; $res_Omega (w)$ is $w$ with each live loan replaced by the resolved content of its borrow.

*Well-formedness.* A state is well formed when (WF1) each label has at most one borrow, which is the whole value of a binding or temporary, never inside data, a sealed program, a closure or a type; (WF2) every loan's borrow is in Ω, except a loan inside a sealed program whose borrow is outside the run (an _inert_ loan); outside sealed programs a label occurs as a loan at most once, inside them any number of times; (WF3) the relation "a loan of ℓ occurs in the content of borrow m" is acyclic; (WF4) closures and Π-closures capture no borrow and no `⊥`, and sealed programs are closed; (WF5) at every call point each argument is loan-free or is $"borrow"_ell u$ with $u$ loan-free; (WF6) each binding's value is `⊥`, or a loan whose borrow's content has the binding's type, or a value of the binding's type, and every σ the state names (in a value, a stored type, the goal or a record) has a type in Δ.

== The machine, closing off and sealed programs <sec-machine>

The machine is big-step and deterministic: $cfg(Omega, t) ev cfg(Omega', v)$, or the run is _stuck_ (it must inspect a neutral), or it fails. Calls evaluate their arguments left to right into temporaries, push a frame binding the parameters, run the body, and pop the frame, dropping its bindings. Types and proofs are _erased_: an erased term runs on a private copy of the state, which is discarded (@sec-erasure).

*Closing off* ([Close]). When a call $f(overline(w))$ is stuck, the call itself returns. Let the borrow arguments be $w_i = "borrow"_(ell_i) u_i$ ($i in I$) with each $u_i$ loan-free, let $L$ be $"let" c_i = u_i$ for $i in I$, and $C$ the call $f(overline(a))$ with $a_i = \&c_i$ for $i in I$ and $a_i = w_i$ otherwise. If $f$'s declared codomain is borrow-free, the call returns #seal[$L; C$] and each $"loan"_(ell_i)$ is filled with #seal[$L; C; c_i$]. If it is $\&T$, the call returns $"borrow"_k seal(L"; let" r = C"; " *r)$ for a fresh $k$, and each $"loan"_(ell_i)$ is filled with #seal[$L; "let" r = C; *r := "loan"_k; c_i$]: the fills have a _hole_ $"loan"_k$ for the final content the caller will write through the returned borrow, which the caller's eventual [End] of $"borrow"_k$ supplies by substitution. A result whose declared type at the call reads as `Unit` is `()`, and so is the fill of a lent place whose declared type is `Unit`. A call whose head is a neutral is stuck at once and closed off the same way.

*Sealed programs.* Values are kept in normal form. A sealed program's normal form is its run from the empty environment, except that its head call $C$ may not itself close off ([Seal]'s head guard); so a sealed program either runs to a value or stays exactly as it is. A loan whose borrow lives outside the run is inert: the run treats it like an abstract value.

*Stuck blocks.* A stuck `match` that is not the body of a call is closed off as a call of an anonymous function of the places it uses. Pattern variables are first resolved to the places they denote; the block takes each maximal place by move if some arm moves it, by borrow if some arm writes or borrows it, and by copy otherwise. A stuck `J` is closed off the same way, with its transported term as the block's one arm. A match on a value of a type with one constructor (a projection `p.i`, a destructuring `let`, a one-arm match) is never closed off in a typed run (D93): an unknown scrutinee is refined in place to its constructor (a sealed one generalised first) and the one arm runs directly, in tail position too. A block's captures are refined in place the same way after its entry and before its arms, so a captured field is a place when the block's call takes it; a fill over a live borrow is left as it is (the entry has ended that borrow).

*Drop at the join* ([Join]). A match leaves moved, on every path, what any of its arms moves: after it, the moved places are the join of the arms' end states (moved ⊔ whole = moved), each arm's net of its own refills. This holds for a stuck block, for a match on a known constructor in a direct run, and for `J`'s transported term, its one arm. A match on a value of a type with one constructor has one arm and so no other path to agree with: it has no join, and no entry, on any path (D93), and leaves exactly what its arm leaves. The checker therefore reads a field directly, as the model does. The moved places are read from syntax and declared types (`Decide.jdMoves`), never from a run: the places an arm reads whose declared type is not a copy type, the borrow variables it reads whole, and every variable a function or Π-type in the arm captures. Their _grain_ is also read from declared types (`Decide.jdGrain`): a move is of a field only through a value whose declared type reads as an inductive with one constructor, not computed by a match; through anything else (several constructors, a type computed by a match, a type parameter, a number's predecessor) it is of the value whole. So the generic call, every instance and every closed run move exactly the same places, and `⊥` means _moved_ on every path.

== Observation, footprints, `Id` and `Eq` <sec-obs>

The _footprint_ $W_Omega (t, u)$ is the set of owners of the roots of the places that $t$ or $u$ borrows or assigns, and of the borrow variables they read whole, in a canonical order read off the syntax. A place that is only read contributes nothing. The owner of a variable holding a borrow is the owners of its loan; of an owned variable, the variable itself.

The _observation_ $obs(t, w: W, s: Omega)$ runs $t$ on a private copy of Ω, ends every remaining borrow, and returns the tuple of the result and the contents of the positions of $W$. A borrow-typed result is observed after writing a given fresh $sigma_w$ through it, so that its owners show where it points ([Obs-borrow]); only conversion observes such results.

$ty("Id")(A, t, u)$ computes, at Ω, to $"and"("eq"(A, r, r'), "eq"(T_1, w_1, w'_1), dots)$, where $obs(t, w: W, s: Omega) = (r, overline(w))$, $obs(u, w: W, s: Omega) = (r', overline(w'))$ and $W = W_Omega (t, u)$; $A$ must be borrow-free. `Eq` computes through the smart constructor `eq`: on two values of one constructor it is the conjunction of the fields' equations (injectivity; for a _dependent_ constructor, one some of whose fields' types mention earlier _index_ fields, only when the index fields are convertible), on values of different constructors of one inductive it is `False` (disjointness), on convertible values (equal by definitional equality, @sec-conv) it is `True`, on `⊥` it compares `⊥` as a value of its own, $"eq"(⊥, ⊥) = ty("True")$ and $"eq"(⊥, v) = ty("False")$ for any other $v$ (an abstract value or a sealed program stands for a value, never for a moved place), and otherwise it is the stuck type $ty("Eq") T space v space w$. `and` drops `True` conjuncts. $ty("J")(A, a, b, P, h, t)$ computes to $t$ only when $a equiv b$; otherwise it is stuck and closed off.

*Type-states* ([Id-typestate]). $ty("Id")(A, t, u)$ is well formed only if $t$ and $u$ leave the same _type-state_, $"TS"(t) = "TS"(u)$, read from their syntax and the declared types of the bindings outside them (`Decide.typeState`), at the frame where the `Id` is formed. Its four components are sets of places at [Join]'s grain, compared as sets: the owned places left _moved_; the borrow bindings _consumed_ (moved whole, passed whole to a call, bound by a `let`, returned, captured, or taken by a match; a reborrow $\&*y$ consumes nothing); the borrow bindings _re-pointed_ (assigned whole and not consumed); and the places behind a borrow _left open_ (moved through $*y$ and not refilled). And neither side may end a borrow that a binding or a temporary of the frame held when the `Id` was formed (the _no-ending clause_). So what a program may do after one side, as far as moves and borrows go, it may do after the other.

*Observing where a borrow points* ([Obs-ref]). For each borrow binding $y : \&T$ in the re-pointed set, one fresh abstract value $sigma_y : T$ is made, and each side's observation writes it through $y$ after the side runs. Every place a side borrows has its owners in the footprint, so the observation shows where $y$ points after each side: `Id(Unit, (y := &x; ()), (y := &z; ()))` computes to $ty("Eq")(ty("Nat"), sigma_y, x) and ty("Eq")(ty("Nat"), z, sigma_y)$, which is not provable. The set is read from syntax and declared types.

== Erasure and private copies <sec-erasure>

A term's _declared type_ is read from the declared types of its heads without normalising (a variable's declared type, a call's callee codomain term, a constructor's inductive, an annotation, a tail's or arms'). A term's _declared sort_ is the sort of its declared type, read the same way. A term is _erased_ when it is a proof (its declared type has declared sort `Prop`) or forms a type (a type former, a call of a function returning a sort, or anything in a _type position_: an annotation, a Π domain or codomain, a type slot of `Eq`, `Id` or `J`, or a parameter or field type). An erased term is a _statement_. A run is in _erased mode_ while it runs a statement, and in _runtime mode_ while it runs code. A Π-type's _class_ (returns types, returns proofs, other) and whether it returns a borrow are read from its codomain term when it is formed, and Π-types that differ in either are not convertible. _Sorts are syntactic_: whatever is written in a type position must have a declared type that is syntactically a sort, so a type's declared sort is its computed sort. Every erased term runs on its own private copy of the state, which is discarded. In the checker these decisions are functions of syntax, declarations and declared types only, collected in one module (`Decide.lean`) that cannot run the machine.

== Typing <sec-typing>

The typing judgement $Omega tack.r t ev v : A tack.l Omega'$ is the machine run with types: each rule performs the machine's step and computes a type. Where the machine would be stuck on the program being checked, typing splits instead. The rules the plan relies on:

- *[Call-type].* The type of a call is its callee's codomain evaluated, on a private copy, with the parameters bound to the arguments' values; each argument's type must convert to the corresponding parameter type, evaluated the same way.
- *[T-Call].* The call's type is its [Call-type]; then the callee runs in the machine, where a stuck body closes off. For a proof-typed call ([T-Call-proof]) the body is never run.
- *[Rec].* In the body of $kw("fix") f dots kw("by") x_j$, $f$ occurs only as the head of a call (also inside nested functions and block arms), and every call of $f$, recognised by the name $f$ binds for itself or a nested function's capture of that name, passes in position $j$ a strict subterm (through any field, and through the borrow for $\&ty("D")$) of the _entry value_ of $x_j$ (its value when the call started), as refined so far.
- *[Split].* A match on a place whose content is an abstract value σ checks each arm $i$ under the refinement $sigma := ty("C")_i (overline(sigma)_i)$ with fresh field values typed by the constructor's telescope; the refinement is applied to the environment, every stored type, Δ and the goal, with sealed programs re-normalised. Then the match is closed off as a stuck block, and checking continues once from the unrefined state.
- *[Split-gen].* A match on a sealed program $n$ first _generalises_ it: replace $n$ everywhere by a fresh σ of the place's type, record $n := sigma$ (so a later derivation of $n$ normalises to σ), then split σ. $kw("split") f kw("in") t$ does the same for the first stuck call of $f$ in the goal.
- *Worlds.* Each arm of a split is checked in its own _world_. Leaving an arm discards everything it established except fresh-name counters and the types of abstract values it created. A generalisation record, and an _η-refinement_ (refining σ to $ty("C")(overline(sigma))$ because σ's type has only the one constructor $ty("C")$), lives until the arm that made it ends: the constructor follows from the type alone, but the fields' types are the arm's.
- *[Def].* A definition is checked at its _generic call_: each borrow parameter is bound to $"borrow"_(ell_i) sigma_i$ with a fresh owned cell $c_i |-> "loan"_(ell_i)$ in a bottom frame, each proof parameter to `⋆`, each other parameter to a fresh $sigma_i$. The goal $G$ is the [Call-type] of that call. The body is checked in _tail position_ (where only trailing drops follow: the body itself, and the tails of `let`s, sequences and arms in tail position), producing a finite _case tree_ whose leaves $(Omega', v, A)$ are the states, values and types at the end of each path; on every leaf the frame's pop must succeed and $A equiv G'$, the goal refined along that path. The tail rules are [Tail-let] and [Tail-seq] (run a `let` or sequence, then continue), [Tail-match] (a match on a known constructor), [Tail-split] (a split on an abstract value, one subtree per arm), [Tail-gen] (generalise a sealed program, then split), [Tail-prop] (a match on a proof, one subtree per arm) and [Tail-end] (a leaf). Constants are checked from the empty state ([Const]); inductive declarations by [Ind-decl], which checks the conditions of @sec-model-conditions.
- *Matches on proofs.* A match whose arms name constructors of an inductive in `Prop` is decided by the scrutinee's type, never its content (`⋆`). It may produce data or a type only if the inductive is a _subsingleton_ (no constructors, or one whose fields are all proofs). `match h {}` on $h : ty("False")$ is ex falso; in tail position it contributes no leaves.

#explainer[large elimination and subsingletons][_Eliminating_ a value means taking it apart with a match. Matching on data is unrestricted. Matching on a proof to produce data or a type is called _large elimination_, and it needs care, because proofs are erased and every two proofs of one proposition count as equal (_proof irrelevance_). If the result could depend on which constructor the proof used, two "equal" proofs would give different results. For example, if $ty("Or")(P, Q)$ could be matched to produce a `Bool`, the proofs $ty("Inl")(t)$ and $ty("Inr")(t)$ of `Or(True, True)` are equal yet would give `true` and `false`, so `true = false`. Large elimination is therefore allowed only from a _subsingleton_, a proposition with at most one possible proof: no constructors (`False`, where there is nothing to match) or one constructor whose fields are all proofs (`True`, `And`, an equation). Lean has the same rule.]

*Dependent fields.* A field whose type mentions earlier fields makes its constructor _dependent_; the earlier fields it mentions are _index fields_. Writing, borrowing or moving a field of a dependent value _opens_ it ([Open]): while open, a data field is typed by its content, assigning it is a strong update, and a write or borrow through an index field sets the proof fields whose type mentions it to `⊥` (an error to read until reassigned). A value must be _whole_ again (every dependent constructor inside it at its telescope type, no `⊥` inside) wherever it passes to code that relies on its type ([Repack]); a part still lent at that point is checked as an unknown of its borrow's type, since the borrow may still write anything of that type there. Where a checked body returns, its result and every place it holds by borrow must be whole ([Return]); [Def] and closures check this at their body's return, and a stuck block at the end of each arm (it is an anonymous function, so its arms are its bodies).

== Conversion <sec-conv>

Conversion $v equiv w$ is the _least_ relation closed under: [Conv-refl] identity up to bound-variable names; [Conv-cong] same outermost former with pairwise convertible components (a constructor value's recorded parameters ignored; sealed programs compared by their program text); [Conv-unit] $ty("And")(ty("True"), P) equiv P equiv ty("And")(P, ty("True"))$; [Conv-pi] two Π-closures with the same class and borrow flag whose parameter types and codomains, evaluated at the generic arguments of the first, are pairwise convertible; [Conv-fun] two function values whose Π-types are convertible, whose captured values are pairwise convertible, and whose generic calls have convertible observations (with one shared $sigma_w$ for a borrow result). There is no η-rule; values are `()` wherever their declared type reads as `Unit`, and every proof is `⋆`.

== Refinements and the two paths <sec-two-paths>

#thm([Definition], [refinement], [
  A _refinement_ α assigns values to abstract values, $sigma |-> v$. It _respects_ a state Ω when each σ it refines is an abstract value of Ω and $alpha(sigma)$ is borrow-free and has a type convertible with Δ(σ). It is applied to a state, value or term by substituting and then restoring normal form: every sealed program that changed is re-run ([Seal]) and every `Eq` type rebuilt by `eq`. We write $Omega alpha$, $v alpha$, $t alpha$; α _covers_ Ω when it assigns a value to every abstract value of Ω. More generally, $Omega[w slash a]^+$ replaces every occurrence of an abstract value, loan or sealed program $a$ by $w$ in the values of Ω, its stored types, Δ and the goal, and then restores normal form; $"nf"(v)$ is the normal form of $v$.
]) <def-refinement>

Refinements cover the three ways the checker instantiates an unknown: a case split ($sigma := ty("C")(overline(sigma)')$), a call site (a parameter's generic σ instantiated with the argument), and an abstract function instantiated with a closure. Borrow-freeness costs nothing: a borrow parameter's generic value is $"borrow"_ell sigma$ with σ borrow-free, and since `&` is never produced by computation and never an argument, no unknown type is ever refined to a borrow type.

*The two paths.* Every statement is evaluated along two paths: at a definition's generic call, where calls and matches on abstract values close off, and directly at each instance, where they run. [Call-type] and [Split] identify the results, so a proof checked on the first path is used on the second. If the paths take any decision differently, and the decision changes a result, a statement proved on one path can be false on the other: a closed proof of `False`. The checker's design principle is that _no decision that changes a result is read from a normal form_. This plan needs less: a decision may read anything, provided its outcomes mean the same, are chosen by syntax the model reads the same way, or are ordered by information (@sec-wf).

The plan does not compare the two paths. It reads the first path's states under _assignments_ (@def-assign), which give each unknown a mathematical element of its type's meaning rather than a machine value.

= The target <sec-target>

#thm([Definition], [accepted program], [
  A _program_ is a finite list of inductive declarations and definitions, read in order. It is _accepted_ when each item is accepted by [Ind-decl], [Def] or [Const] against the signature of the items before it, the library's declarations (`Pair`, `False`, `True`, `And`, with `Nat` and `Unit` built in) coming first. It is _hole-free_ when no term in it is a hole (`?`, also written `sorry`). Every definition has a body, so holes are the only axioms.
]) <def-accepted>

#target([consistency of the calculus], [
  Assume ZFC with one inaccessible cardinal per universe level used (both defined in @sec-sets). For every accepted, hole-free program, no definition without parameters has a type that evaluates to `False`. Equivalently, no closed term has type `False`, nor any type, such as `Eq(Nat, Z, S(Z))`, that computes to it.
]) <target>

#target([type safety], [
  Let $cal(P)$ be an accepted, hole-free program, $d$ an accepted _data function_ of $cal(P)$ (its codomain is data or a borrow type), and $d(overline(v))$ a _closed call_: every argument is a closed value _built by accepted code_ (a constructor value, or a closure or global of $cal(P)$, holding no unknown and no sealed program), every borrow argument is a borrow of a cell holding such a value, and every proof parameter's type, evaluated at the earlier arguments, is _true_ in the set model. Then the _erased run_ of the call (the erased program's run: the machine in runtime mode, with statements not run, every proof `⋆`, and $ty("J")(A, a, b, P, h, t)$ evaluating to $t$ unconditionally by [J-run], D85; its entry point is `runCall` in `Ochr/Runtime.lean`) terminates; raises no error and never gets stuck; returns, if $d$ returns a borrow, a live borrow whose content is whole; and leaves a result and final contents of the borrowed cells that mean what the reference run of the call says (@def-refrun).
]) <target-safe>

*Why two.* The plan serves the claim that a language like Ochr makes a good general-purpose language. Its proofs must be trustworthy, which is @target, and its programs must not crash or go wrong when run, which is @target-safe. An earlier draft added a third target, `Id` adequacy (terms proved equal by `Id` can be swapped in any context, with a resource lemma for when acceptance carries over); it was dropped on 13 November 2026 because neither guarantee needs it. `ochr/docs/30-id-adequacy-audit.md` keeps the audit. No Lean statement of @target-safe is fixed yet.

*Why "built by accepted code".* A closure from outside the program could loop or go wrong; arguments built by the program's accepted code behave as their meanings say (@sec-safety, termination). *Why "true", not "proved".* It is the stronger statement: it covers a caller that obtains its hypotheses outside Ochr. It is also why `J` at runtime must not decide by conversion.

*Termination.* Ochr stays total for now: every recursion passes [Rec], so type safety includes termination. General recursion is desirable, but is deferred until a pure, consistent and sound core exists.

The Lean statement of @target against the checker is `Ochr.Consistency.consistency`:
```
theorem consistency (q : Program) (fuel : Nat)
    (holes : NoHoles q) (acc : Accepted (Prelude.decls ++ q) fuel) :
    ∀ d ∈ q, ¬ d.IsFalseConst
```
`Prelude.decls` is the library's declarations. `Accepted p fuel` says that the checker's entry point `checkProgram` gives every declaration of `p` the verdict _accepted_ at that fuel (not _rejected_, and not _undecided_, which is what running out of fuel or a depth limit gives). `IsFalseConst` says a declaration is a definition with no parameters whose declared type is the identifier `False`.

*How the two statements relate.*
- *Rules and checker.* @target is about the rules; the Lean statement is about the checker that implements them. Every step of this plan is phrased so that it can be stated against the checker's functions, so a completed plan would prove both, the Lean form being the stronger since it also excludes implementation errors.
- *A literal `False` loses nothing.* If an accepted program had a parameterless $c$ whose type merely computes to an empty type, one more fresh declaration turns it into a literal one: `def B : False := c` when the type computes to `False`; a one-arm match for `False ∧ ⊤`; an annotated zero-arm match for a user's empty inductive. The Lean statement quantifies over all programs, so it covers these.
- *Fuel.* Exhaustion is an outcome of its own that no rule below the verdict catches, and a caught failure cannot refund fuel, so a decided verdict is the same at every larger fuel. The Lean statement quantifies over every fuel and does not need this fact, but a proof may use it to fix a fuel large enough for a given run.
- *Totality.* Every function the checker runs is total, with explicit fuel where recursion is not structural, so the kernel can unfold every step. The one exception, an opaque address used to key the editor's location table, is never consulted when locations are off, which `Accepted` ensures.
- *Universes.* Lean's strength is ZFC with $n$ inaccessibles for every $n < omega$. If the set model for Ochr needs one inaccessible per level at once, the uniform Lean statement is unprovable in Lean (Gödel's second incompleteness theorem: a theory cannot prove the consistency of one at least as strong, @sec-sets), and must be weakened to bounded universe levels, made relative to an assumed model, or stated per program (@sec-oq, Q1).

#explainer[Lean's kernel and its axioms][Lean's _kernel_ is the small trusted core that checks every proof; tactics and the elaborator only produce proof terms for it to check, so a Lean theorem is believed exactly as far as the kernel is. Beyond its core type theory, Lean has three standard axioms: `propext` (two propositions that imply each other are equal), `Quot.sound` (elements related by a relation are equal in its quotient) and `Classical.choice` (from a proof that something exists, pick one). All three hold in Lean's set model. `#print axioms t` lists the axioms a theorem $t$ depends on; an unfinished proof shows up there as `sorryAx`. A `partial def` is opaque to the kernel: it can run, but a proof cannot unfold it, which is why a provable statement about the checker needs every function the checker runs to be total.]

= The shape of the argument <sec-shape>

The argument has three parts: a set model that says what "true" means (@sec-model); a meaning for every state the checker reaches, with a theorem that the checker's steps keep it (@sec-route); and consistency as a corollary (@cor-cons-route). Type safety follows from the same theorem and four further lemmas (@sec-safety).

== The set model <sec-model>

=== Background: sets, universes and models <sec-sets>

This subsection defines everything the model uses. It follows the standard set model of Lean (Carneiro, _The Type Theory of Lean_, a copy of which is at `docs/papers/theory-of-lean.pdf`), but nothing below depends on having read it. For readers who want the full construction for Lean, of which @sec-model is an adaptation, the relevant parts are, optionally: §1.2 (why sets model a type theory, the two-element `Prop`, and why inaccessible cardinals are needed); §6.2 (the interpretation map, with universes $U_(n+1) = V_(kappa_n)$ and the clauses for Π, ∀ and `=`); §6.2.1 (the least-fixed-point construction of inductive types in ZFC); and §6.3, Theorem 6.7 (the soundness of the model: types denote sets, proofs denote •, convertible terms denote the same thing).

*ZFC, and consistency relative to it.* ZFC is Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the axiom of choice, the usual foundation of mathematics. A theory T is _consistent_ if it does not prove a contradiction. By Gödel's second incompleteness theorem, a consistent theory cannot prove its own consistency, nor that of a theory at least as strong; so a consistency proof for a strong type theory is always _relative_: "if ZFC plus some axioms is consistent, so is the type theory".

#explainer[countable and uncountable sizes][Two sets have the _same size_ if there is a bijection between them (a pairing that matches every element of one with exactly one element of the other); $|A|$ is the size of $A$, and $|A| <= |B|$ means $A$ can be matched one-to-one into part of $B$. A set is _countable_ if it is finite or has the same size as the natural numbers $NN$, that is, its elements can be listed $a_0, a_1, a_2, dots$; the integers, the rationals and the set of all finite strings are countable. A set is _uncountable_ if it is not. Cantor's theorem says that no set $A$ has the same size as $cal(P)(A)$, the set of all its subsets: $cal(P)(A)$ is always strictly bigger. So $cal(P)(NN)$, equivalently the real numbers or the set of all functions $NN -> NN$, is uncountable, and the sizes go on growing forever: $NN$, $cal(P)(NN)$, $cal(P)(cal(P)(NN))$, …]

#explainer[cardinals][A _cardinal_ is a size. The size of $NN$ is written $aleph_0$ ("aleph-nought"), the smallest infinite cardinal; the cardinals bigger than it are the _uncountable_ cardinals. If $lambda = |A|$, then $2^lambda$ is the size of $cal(P)(A)$ (a subset of $A$ is a choice of in or out for each element, hence the notation); by Cantor, $2^lambda > lambda$ always. Cardinals are ordered by $<=$ above, and "fewer than $kappa$ sets" means a collection of them whose size is less than $kappa$.]

#explainer[ordinals][Ordinals extend counting past the natural numbers. They measure _position_ in a sequence, not size: $0, 1, 2, dots, omega, omega + 1, omega + 2, dots, omega dot 2, dots$, where ω is the first position after every natural number. Every ordinal other than 0 is either a _successor_, which has an immediate predecessor ($3 = 2 + 1$; $omega + 1$ comes straight after ω), or a _limit_, which has none and is reached only as the limit of all smaller ordinals (ω, $omega dot 2$, …). Different ordinals can have the same size: ω, $omega + 1$ and $omega dot 2$ are different positions but all countable. Each cardinal is identified with the first ordinal of that size, so $aleph_0$ is ω, and every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, though most limit ordinals are not cardinals. This identification is what lets a cardinal $kappa$ index a stage $V_kappa$ below.]

#thm([Definition], [cumulative hierarchy], [
  $V_0 = emptyset$; $V_(alpha + 1) = cal(P)(V_alpha)$, the set of all subsets of $V_alpha$; and $V_lambda = union.big_(alpha < lambda) V_alpha$ for a limit ordinal λ. Every set belongs to some $V_alpha$.
]) <def-V>

#thm([Definition], [inaccessible cardinal], [
  An uncountable cardinal $kappa$ is _inaccessible_ if it is _regular_ (a union of fewer than $kappa$ sets, each of size less than $kappa$, has size less than $kappa$) and a _strong limit_ ($2^lambda < kappa$ whenever $lambda < kappa$).
]) <def-inacc>

The two conditions say that $kappa$ cannot be reached from below by the two ways of making sets bigger: taking a union of fewer than $kappa$ smaller sets, and taking all subsets of a smaller set. $aleph_0$ satisfies both (a finite union of finite sets is finite, and a finite set has finitely many subsets), which is why the definition asks for an uncountable $kappa$: an inaccessible is to the uncountable cardinals below it what $aleph_0$ is to the finite numbers.

If $kappa$ is inaccessible, $V_kappa$ is closed under every construction a type theory needs: for $A in V_kappa$ and a family $B(x) in V_kappa$ indexed by $x in A$, the set of subsets of $A$, the _dependent sum_ $Sigma(x in A) B(x)$ (the set of pairs $(x, y)$ with $x in A$ and $y in B(x)$; when $A$ is a set of tags this is a _disjoint union_, written $union.big.plus_(x in A) B(x)$) and the _dependent product_ $product_(x in A) B(x)$ (defined below) are again in $V_kappa$. So $V_kappa$ can serve as "the type of all small types". ZFC cannot prove that an inaccessible exists (with one, $V_kappa$ would be a model of ZFC, proving ZFC consistent), so the existence of inaccessibles is the extra axiom the target assumes.

*Notation.* Throughout, $deno(T)$ is the set a type $T$ is interpreted as, and $deno(v)$ the element a value $v$ is interpreted as.

*Universes.* Fix inaccessibles $kappa_0 < kappa_1 < dots$, one for each universe level used. $ty("Type")_i$ is interpreted as $V_(kappa_i)$: a type in $ty("Type")_i$ is a set in $V_(kappa_i)$. Since $V_(kappa_i) in V_(kappa_(i+1))$, the rule $ty("Type")_i : ty("Type")_(i+1)$ holds in the model.

*Propositions, proofs and proof irrelevance.* Fix an object •, "the proof". A proposition is interpreted as $emptyset$ (false) or ${bullet}$ (true), so `Prop` is interpreted as the two-element set ${emptyset, {bullet}}$, and every proof is interpreted as •. Any two proofs of one proposition therefore have the same meaning; this _proof irrelevance_ is what makes it sound for the calculus to represent every proof by the single value `⋆`. A _subsingleton_ is a set with at most one element; every proposition denotes one.

*Dependent products.* A function type $Pi(x : A). B$ whose codomain is data or a type is interpreted as the dependent product $product_(x in deno(A)) deno(B)(x)$: the set of functions $f$ with domain $deno(A)$ such that $f(x) in deno(B)(x)$ for every $x$.

*Products of propositions, and impredicativity.* When $B$ is a proposition the product cannot be used as it stands, because it is not a truth value, but it is always one step away from one. Each $deno(B)(x)$ is $emptyset$ or ${bullet}$, so there are two cases. If $deno(B)(x_0) = emptyset$ for some $x_0$, no function can choose a value at $x_0$, and the product is $emptyset$. If $deno(B)(x) = {bullet}$ for every $x$, each $f(x)$ is forced to be $bullet$, so exactly one function qualifies, the constant function $x |-> bullet$ (when $deno(A)$ is empty, it is the empty function), and the product is ${x |-> bullet}$. So the product always has at most one element: it is empty exactly when "$deno(B)(x)$ is true for every $x$" fails. But its one element, when there is one, is a function, not $bullet$, so the product is a singleton without being the set ${bullet}$. The model therefore interprets a Π into a proposition not as the product but as the truth value the product carries:
$ deno(Pi(x : A). B) = { bullet | deno(B)(x) = {bullet} "for every" x in deno(A) }, $
which is ${bullet}$ when the product is a singleton and $emptyset$ when it is empty. This collapse loses nothing, and it is forced by proof irrelevance: every proof denotes $bullet$, so a proof of $Pi(x : A). B$, for instance a `λ`, must denote $bullet$ too rather than the function $x |-> bullet$, and applying such a proof to an argument denotes $bullet$, which is what $deno(B)(x)$'s one element is. Since no rule can look inside a proof, nothing can tell the collapsed meaning from the product it replaces. The result is always $emptyset$ or ${bullet}$, a member of `Prop`'s interpretation ${emptyset, {bullet}}$, _however large_ $deno(A)$ is, even when $A$ is `Prop` itself or a universe. This is _impredicativity_: a proposition may quantify over all propositions, or over all types of a universe, without moving to a larger universe. The set model gets it for free because the size of the domain never reaches the result. The same move is unavailable for data: `Π(A : Type₀). A → A` is a set of functions on all of $V_(kappa_0)$, which is too large to be a member of $V_(kappa_0)$ again, so a Π over a universe into data must live in a larger universe.

*Least fixed points.* An operator Φ on families of sets is _monotone_ if $X subset.eq Y$ (pointwise) implies $Phi(X) subset.eq Phi(Y)$. Its _least fixed point_ is the smallest family $X$ with $Phi(X) subset.eq X$, the intersection of all such families, which exists for a monotone operator acting inside a fixed set such as $V_kappa$. An inductive type is interpreted as the least fixed point of the operator "one more layer of constructors". In Ochr no recursive occurrence of a type lies under a Π (fields are first-order), so every element is built by finitely many constructor applications, and the least fixed point is the union $union.big_(n < omega) Phi^n (emptyset)$, which stays inside $V_kappa$.

*Why a model gives consistency.* A model assigns to each type $T$ a set $deno(T)$ and to each well-typed term $t : T$ an element $deno(t) in deno(T)$; that it does so for every derivable typing is the model's _soundness_. Since $deno(ty("False")) = emptyset$, no closed term of type `False` can exist, and since the model is built in ZFC with the inaccessibles, the conclusion is relative to that theory. For Ochr, "well-typed term" is replaced by "definition accepted by the checker", and the model's soundness is @cor-mean, which follows from the theorem of @sec-route that the checker's steps keep meaning.

=== The interpretation

Types are interpreted as sets in the way just described, adapting Werner's model of the calculus of inductive constructions (Coq's type theory) and Carneiro's of Lean. As above, $deno(T)$ is the meaning of a closed type value $T$, and $deno(v)$ of a closed value.

#thm([Definition], [meaning of types], [
  - $deno(ty("Type")_i) = V_(kappa_i)$ and $deno(ty("Prop")) = {emptyset, {bullet}}$.
  - An inductive declaration in `Type₀` denotes, simultaneously for every value of its parameters, the least family of sets closed under its constructors: the least fixed point of the operator sending a family $X$ to $ā |-> union.big.plus_i {ty("C")_i} times Sigma(g_1 : B_1) dots Sigma(g_k : B_k)$, where each field type is read at the parameters $ā$ and the earlier fields, with every occurrence $ty("D")(overline(b))$ read as $X(overline(b))$. Non-uniform occurrences make the fixed point simultaneous over the whole family; it is still the least fixed point of a monotone operator on families.

    _For example_, `inductive List (A : Type) := Nil | Cons(h : A, t : List(A))` has, at a parameter value $T$, the operator
    $ Phi(X) = {ty("Nil")} union.plus ({ty("Cons")} times deno(T) times X), $
    writing $ty("Cons")(a, l)$ for the triple $(ty("Cons"), a, l)$. Its iterates from $emptyset$ are $Phi^0(emptyset) = emptyset$; $Phi^1(emptyset) = {ty("Nil")}$, since `Nil` has no fields and a `Cons` needs an element of $X = emptyset$; $Phi^2(emptyset)$ adds the one-element lists $ty("Cons")(a, ty("Nil"))$ for $a in deno(T)$; and in general $Phi^(n+1)(emptyset)$ is exactly the lists of length at most $n$, each stage containing the one before (by induction, since Φ is monotone). Their union $L = union.big_(n < omega) Phi^n (emptyset)$ is therefore exactly the finite lists of elements of $deno(T)$: a list of length $n$ appears at stage $n + 1$, and every stage holds only finite lists. $L$ is a fixed point. An element of $Phi(L)$ is `Nil`, which is in $L$, or $ty("Cons")(a, l)$ with $l in L$, so $l$ is in some stage $Phi^n (emptyset)$ and $ty("Cons")(a, l)$ in the next. Conversely an element of $L$ lies in some $Phi^(n+1)(emptyset) = Phi(Phi^n (emptyset)) subset.eq Phi(L)$, by monotonicity. And $L$ is the least such set: if $Phi(Y) subset.eq Y$, as for any fixed point $Y$, then every stage is inside $Y$, by induction on the stage, since $emptyset subset.eq Y$, and $Phi^n (emptyset) subset.eq Y$ gives $Phi^(n+1)(emptyset) subset.eq Phi(Y) subset.eq Y$. So $L subset.eq Y$, and $deno(ty("List")(T)) = L$.
  - An inductive declaration in `Prop` denotes the same fixed point, truncated: ${bullet}$ if inhabited, $emptyset$ otherwise. So $deno(ty("False")) = emptyset$, $deno(ty("True")) = {bullet}$ and $deno(ty("And")(P, Q)) = deno(P) inter deno(Q)$.
  - $deno(ty("Eq")(A, a, b)) = {bullet}$ if $deno(a) = deno(b)$, and $emptyset$ otherwise. An equation field denotes the same.
  - A function type with borrow parameters $x_i : \&T_i$ for $i in I$ denotes a subset $R$ (defined next, @def-inj) of $Pi(overline(d) : overline(D)). "Out"_B (overline(d))$, where $D_i = deno(T_i)$ for $i in I$ and $D_j = deno(A_j)$ otherwise, $"Fin" = product_(i in I) deno(T_i)$ is the tuple of final contents of the borrowed places, and $"Out"_B (overline(d))$ is $deno(B)(overline(d))$ if $B$ is a proposition, $deno(B)(overline(d)) times "Fin"$ if $B$ is borrow-free data, and $deno(T) times (deno(T) -> "Fin")$ if $B = \&T$. For $F$ in this set, $"fwd"_F$ and $"back"_F$ are the two components of $F(overline(d))$; $"back"_F (overline(d))$ is the _backward function_, mapping the value the caller leaves in the returned borrow to the final contents. A Π-type whose class is "returns proofs" is the exception: as argued in @sec-sets, it denotes not this set but the truth value it carries, ${bullet}$ if $deno(B)(overline(d)) = {bullet}$ for every $overline(d)$ and $emptyset$ otherwise (impredicativity). Its borrowed places need no final contents, since a proof's run is discarded.

    _Example, a function that takes a borrow._ In-place addition, `def AddM (x : &Nat) (y : Nat) : Unit`, which walks down `*x` to its final `Z` and replaces it with `y`. Its one borrow parameter is `x`, so $I = {x}$, both domains are $deno(ty("Nat")) = NN$, and $"Fin" = NN$ is the final content of `*x`. The codomain `Unit` is borrow-free data, so a meaning is a function $NN times NN -> deno(ty("Unit")) times NN$, and `AddM` denotes $(n, m) |-> ((), n + m)$: started with `*x` holding $n$, it returns `()` and leaves $n + m$ in `*x`. The borrow is thus modelled as passing the place's starting content in and getting its final content back out.

    _Example, a function that returns a borrow._ `def TailM (x : &Nat) : &Nat`, which returns a borrow of the final `Z` inside `*x`. Again $I = {x}$ and $"Fin" = NN$, but now $B = \&ty("Nat")$, so a meaning sends the starting content $n$ of `*x` to a pair in $NN times (NN -> NN)$, and `TailM` denotes $n |-> (0, v |-> n + v)$. The first component, $"fwd"(n) = 0$, is what the returned borrow holds when the call returns: the final `Z`. The second, $"back"(n) = (v |-> n + v)$, is the backward function: if the caller leaves $v$ in the returned borrow before it ends, `*x` finally holds $n$ with its final `Z` replaced by $v$, that is $n + v$. This backward function is injective (different $v$ give different final contents), which is what the injectivity relation defined next demands of every function returning a borrow.
  - $deno(ty("Id")(A, t, u)) = {bullet}$ iff the meanings of the two observations of @sec-obs are equal.
]) <def-meaning>

*What $R$ is for.* For a function that returns a borrow, the backward function says how the final contents of the borrowed places depend on the value the caller leaves in the returned borrow. The plain product of functions contains every pair $("fwd", "back")$, including ones whose backward function ignores its argument entirely. The model must leave those out, because no Ochr function behaves that way and the checker accepts proofs that rely on it. $R$ is the condition that leaves them out: the backward function of every function returning a borrow, at every type, must be _injective_, so that different values left in the returned borrow give different final contents.

*Why every Ochr function has an injective backward function.* A function type returning a borrow must have a borrow parameter, so Ochr has no `'static` borrows: a returned borrow is always derived from one of the function's borrow arguments, and points at some position inside the places they lend. When it ends, the value the caller left in it is put back at that position. So the final contents still hold that value at that position, and two different values give two different final contents. For `TailM` above, $"back"(n) = (v |-> n + v)$: the value left in the borrow becomes the final `Z`'s replacement, and can be read back off the result. A function returning a leaked borrow would be different: Rust allows a `fn(&mut u64) -> &mut u64` to return `Box::leak(Box::new(0))`, a borrow of a fresh place that nobody owns, whose backward function is constant because what is written through it never reaches the caller's places.

*Why the model must build it in.* The checker relies on it, so a model without it would make an accepted program false. Take
```
def PF (x : &Nat) (e : Id(Unit, *x := 0, *x := 1)) : False := e
```
At its generic call the statement observes the owner of `x`, where the two sides leave `0` and `1`, so it computes to $ty("Eq")(ty("Nat"), 0, 1)$, which is `False`, and `PF` is accepted: writing `0` and writing `1` through a borrow can never be indistinguishable. Now call `PF` with a borrow returned by an unknown function $g : Pi(x : \&ty("Nat")). \&ty("Nat")$. Its hypothesis says that writing `0` and writing `1` through $g(x)$ leave the same contents, which in the model says $"back"_g (n)(0) = "back"_g (n)(1)$. If the meaning of that function type contained a $g$ with a constant backward function, this hypothesis would be true for that $g$, and `PF` would turn it into a proof of `False` that is true in the model, which no sound model allows. So the meaning of a function type returning a borrow must contain only functions with injective backward functions, which is $R$.

#thm([Definition], [injectivity relation], [
  For each type $A$, $R_A subset.eq deno(A)$ is the set of values of $A$ all of whose borrow-returning functions, however deeply nested, have injective backward functions. It is defined by induction on $A$. For propositions and universes $R_A = deno(A)$. At an inductive type, $R$ holds of a constructor value when it holds of each field. $R_(\&T) = R_T$. For a function type, $F in R_(Pi(overline(x) : overline(A)). B)$ iff for all arguments in $R$, $"fwd"_F (overline(d)) in R_B$ and, if $B = \&T$, $"back"_F (overline(d))$ is injective.
]) <def-inj>

*Making the definition well founded.* Read naively, @def-meaning and @def-inj refer to each other at function types (the meaning is a subset cut out by $R$, and $R$ a subset of the meaning), and "by induction on $A$" has nothing smaller to recurse on: a function type's codomain is computed by running code at each argument (a type function such as `Cells(E, n)` is defined by recursion on data), and a type variable $A : ty("Type")_0$ has no structure. Both are resolved as follows, and test 0 checked the resolution on a slice (@sec-test-model). A function type means only its good functions, so $R$ at every type is the meaning itself and the two definitions are one; a type variable means a set, all of whose elements are good. Meanings are defined on terms, not on type values, by recursion on the item's position in the program, then the term's structure, then, inside a recursive function, the rank of its decreasing argument; $ty("Type")_i$ means $V_(kappa_i)$ outright. What remains, beyond the slice, is Q2 (@sec-oq).

#thm([Definition], [truth], [
  A closed proposition value $P$ is _true_, $ok(P)$, when $deno(P) = {bullet}$.
]) <def-truth>

Truth is semantic because propositions include Π-types (implication and universal quantification over data, functions and propositions), user inductives in `Prop`, and `Eq` at any type. In particular a stuck equation is true exactly when its two sides mean the same, whatever the checker can compute.

=== Conditions under which the model is defined <sec-model-conditions>

#thm([Lemma], [the model is well defined], [
  If every inductive declaration satisfies (I1)–(I6) below, every match on a proof satisfies (I7), recursion satisfies (I8), `Eq` satisfies (I9), and borrows are only of `Type₀`, then @def-meaning assigns a set to every type value formed by an accepted program, in the universe of its sort.
  - (I1) _Strict positivity._ The declared type $ty("D")$ occurs in a field type only as the whole field type, or inside another inductive at a _nestable_ parameter (one occurring in that inductive's fields only as a whole field type or at a nestable parameter); never left of a Π, under `&`, or as the argument of a type function.
  - (I2) The arguments of an occurrence of $ty("D")$ never mention $ty("D")$.
  - (I3) A parameter is never called in a field type; only earlier global type functions are.
  - (I4) Parameter types do not mention $ty("D")$.
  - (I5) Fields form a telescope: a field's type mentions only parameters and earlier fields.
  - (I6) Every field of an inductive in `Type₀` or `Prop` lives in `Prop` or `Type₀`.
  - (I7) A match on a proof produces data or a type only from a subsingleton (no constructors, or one whose fields are all proofs).
  - (I8) Recursion is never on a proof.
  - (I9) `Eq` decides only by conversion (`True`) or by distinct constructors of one inductive (`False`); otherwise it is stuck. So an equation at any type, functions and sorts included, adds no rule that could prove `False`.
]) <lem-model>
#proof[(I1)–(I5) make the constructor operator monotone on families, so the least fixed point exists; (I6) keeps it in the sort's universe; (I7) is what makes a match on a proof well defined, since all proofs are $bullet$ and only a subsingleton's eliminator cannot tell them apart; (I8) because a proof has no structure to measure; (I9) because constructors are injective with disjoint images. Borrows only of `Type₀`: borrowing a universe would put `Π(x : &Type₀)(a : *x). *x` in `Type₀`, which would be impredicative and have no set model.]

#explainer[strict positivity and W-types][An inductive declaration is read as "the least set closed under its constructors", and such a set exists only if the type being declared is used _positively_ in its fields. A field that is a function _out of_ the type breaks this, and no set fits the declaration. In Ochr, with `inductive Bad := MkBad(f : Π(x : Bad). Empty)`:
  ```
  def L (b : Bad) : Empty := match b { MkBad(f) => f(b) }
  def bad : Bad := MkBad(λ(x : Bad) : Empty => L(x))
  def Boom : False := absurd(L(bad))
  ```
  `L` applies `b`'s own field to `b` itself; `L(bad)` would loop forever, but `Boom` is typed by its callee's statement alone and proofs are not run, so it would be a closed proof of `False`. (Before the positivity rule existed, the checker accepted exactly this.) Linearity does not stop it: `f` is used once, and `b` is consumed only once, by the call, because the match does not consume it (it names `f` as a sub-place of `b`). A language where a match consumes its scrutinee, such as Bend, must rebuild the value and write `f(MkBad(f))`, using `f` twice, which affinity rejects; Ochr's matches do not consume, so it needs positivity instead. _Strict positivity_ is the syntactic rule that rules this out: the type being declared appears only as a whole field type, or nested inside another inductive that itself uses it positively, never left of an arrow. A _W-type_ is an inductive with a field that is a function _into_ the type, such as `Tree := Node(children : Π(n : Nat). Tree)`, an infinitely branching tree. That is strictly positive and Lean allows it, but its least fixed point is reached only after transfinitely many stages of the construction, which the model must have room for. Ochr forbids any function-typed field, so it has no W-types, and its fixed points are reached after ω stages (@sec-sets). This is one reason Ochr may be weaker than Lean (Q1).]

*Status.* #s-paper. Every condition is enforced by [Ind-decl], the match rules and [Rec]; a probe of each condition against the checker found none missing. One condition is enforced more strictly than (I9) needs: equation fields are required to be over first-order data, which a type parameter instantiated at a function type bypasses harmlessly.

== Conversion is sound <sec-conv-sound>

#thm([Lemma], [conversion respects meaning], [
  If $v equiv w$ (definitionally equal) at a state Ω reached by the checker, then $deno(v, at: gamma) = deno(w, at: gamma)$ for every assignment $gamma$ of Ω (@def-assign). In particular, convertible closed values (no free variables and no unknowns, @sec-prelim) have equal meanings, and convertible propositions are equally true.
]) <lem-conv>

The argument is one principle per conversion rule. Every principle reads the values under an arbitrary assignment, so it speaks of every element of every type, not only of values the machine can build.
- (V1) Normal forms are compared structurally; the meaning of a normal form is a function of its parts.
- (V2) A constructor value means its tag and fields; the parameters it records are fixed by its type and never read, so ignoring them is sound.
- (V3) A sealed program means the meaning of its program (@def-state-meaning), so two with the same text have the same meaning under every assignment, and refinement substitutes into both alike.
- (V4) A Π-type means its family of parameter types and codomain over every argument. [Conv-pi] compares the two at the generic arguments of the first, which are fresh unknowns. An assignment extended to those unknowns can give them any element of the parameter types' meanings, so, by @thm-mp applied to the evaluation of the parameter types and the codomain, equal meanings at the generic arguments under every extension are equal meanings at every argument. The class and the borrow flag are part of the meaning: they decide which functions inhabit the type and how their calls close off.
- (V5) A function value means a function on its annotated domain, with the backward functions of @def-meaning. [Conv-fun] compares the observations of the two generic calls, which run each function's body on fresh unknowns. By @thm-mp applied to those runs, the meaning of an observation under every extension of $gamma$ is the function's result and final contents at every argument, since the fresh unknowns range over every element of the domain, non-computable functions included. So convertible observations mean equal functions.
  #why[nothing is compared with a run at an instance: the generic call's own meaning, under every assignment, already covers every argument.]
- (V6) Conversion is least: a comparison that needs itself answers "not convertible". Read coinductively, any two functions stuck at their generic calls would be identified, and `J` along that proves `Eq(Nat, S(Z), Z)`.
- (V7) The unit laws and $eta$ at `Unit` are equalities of sets: $deno(ty("True") and P) = deno(P)$, and `Unit` has one element.
- (V8) `Eq` computes by meanings: reflexivity by (V1)–(V7), injectivity and disjointness by (V2), and `⊥` is compared as a value: it means _moved_, the same on every path by [Join]. `J` returns its argument only at convertible endpoints, where it is the identity on meanings.
Conversion need not be stable under refinement: it is a check made at one state, and @thm-mp carries its conclusion through every later step.

*An induction shared with @thm-mp.* (V4) and (V5) use @thm-mp on runs that conversion makes, and @thm-mp uses this lemma at every leaf. Both are proved by one induction on the checker's derivation, which is finite because the checker accepted: the runs a conversion check makes are parts of that check's derivation.

*Status.* #s-paper. Every rule is enforced by the checker's conversion; 35 probes, among them transport along true equations between in-place programs, behave as predicted.

== Meaning preservation <sec-route>

#reminder[
  - The checker checks a definition $d_k$ once, at its _generic call_, where each parameter is bound to a fresh abstract value ([Def], @sec-typing). A match on an unknown _splits_, checking each arm in its own _world_ ([Split], Worlds); a call that gets stuck on an unknown is _closed off_ into a sealed program #seal[$t$] ([Close], @sec-machine).
  - $deno(T)$ is the set a closed type $T$ means in the model (@def-meaning), and $R$ the injectivity relation (@def-inj).
  - The check of $d_k$ builds a finite _case tree_: a node for each split, with one child per arm, and a leaf where the body ends.
]

=== The idea

The checker never runs a definition on real inputs. It runs it once, on unknowns, and accepts it if every leaf of the case tree has the goal's type. The plan does not run the definition a second time on concrete inputs. It gives each state of that run a meaning, as a function of what the unknowns stand for, and compares the run with the _reference run_ of the same term: the meaning of running it, which applies the meanings of functions at calls instead of running their bodies (@def-refrun). The reference run _simulates_ the checker's run: from any state of meanings related to the checker's state it reaches one related to the checker's next state, where related means the same meanings, the same live borrows and the same places lent to each (@def-sim). At a leaf the checker has established that the result has the goal's type, so under every choice of what the unknowns stand for, the result's meaning lies in the goal's. Since the unknowns range over every element of their types, that is exactly the statement that the definition means an element of its type.

#why[comparing the checker's run with a second, concrete machine run would need a relation between two symbolic states, matching the unknowns of one against the values of the other, and it could not reach a function parameter that no machine value implements. The reference run is not a machine run: it applies meanings, is never stuck, and ranges over every set function. It does carry a state of its own, though, and the two states have to be related. An earlier form of this plan compared them by an order between meanings alone, and that form is false once a borrow has been held (@def-sim). The relation that replaces it has four parts, each forced by a counterexample, and the ways the two runs differ (a label numbered differently, a value in flight) are its cases.]

#explainer[symbolic execution and its soundness][_Symbolic execution_ runs a program on unknowns instead of values. Where the program branches on an unknown, the executor follows every branch, recording on each what it learned (a _path condition_, such as "$sigma = 0$"). Each symbolic state stands for many concrete situations: one for each choice of values for its unknowns that satisfies its path condition. The executor is _sound_ if each symbolic step maps every situation its state stands for to one the next state stands for. The statement is one-sided: a symbolic state may stand for _more_ situations than can really arise (an _over-approximation_), which only makes the executor more cautious. No second run is involved; the comparison is between one step and what the step means. Aeneas, the Rust verification tool whose borrow model Ochr's machine follows, proves its symbolic semantics sound against its concrete one in this style. Here the situations are not machine states but set-theoretic meanings, which is what lets them include non-computable functions.]

=== Assignments and the meaning of a state

#thm([Definition], [assignment], [
  Let Ω be a state the checker reaches while checking a definition. An _assignment_ $gamma$ of Ω gives each abstract value $sigma$ that Ω _references_ an element $gamma(sigma) in deno(Delta(sigma), at: gamma)$, in the order the unknowns were created (a type in Δ may mention earlier unknowns, so its meaning depends on $gamma$ at them). Ω references $sigma$ when $sigma$ occurs in a value of Ω, in a stored or deferred type, in the goal, in a record in force, or in the type of a referenced unknown. No separate condition of injectivity is needed: a type means only its good elements, so $R$ is the meaning itself (@sec-test-model). It must satisfy the _path condition_ of Ω: for each generalisation record $n := sigma$ in force in the current world, $gamma(sigma) = deno(n, at: gamma)$. A refinement made by a split or an η-expansion substitutes a constructor value for $sigma$ throughout the state, but the checker keeps a record of each in its world and reads it back (a sealed program generalised before in the world becomes its σ with the refinements since substituted; an η-refinement gives its fields the same names in every private copy), so each is a path condition too: $gamma(sigma) = deno(r, at: gamma)$ for each refinement $sigma := r$ in force, which the arm's choice of $gamma$ meets. An assignment of $d_k$'s generic state is a choice of arguments for $d_k$: one element of each parameter's type, including the initial content of each borrowed cell.
]) <def-assign>

#why[an assignment owes an element only to the unknowns the state still refers to. Leaving an arm of a split discards the arm's world, but the checker keeps the types of the unknowns the arm created, and such a type can be empty in the world the assignment selects: a field of type `Void`, say, in an arm the assignment does not take. If every recorded unknown were owed an element, no assignment of the state after such a split would exist, and the step for leaving an arm would be false (checked in Lean, @sec-spine).]

Nothing requires $gamma(sigma)$ to be computable. For $sigma : Pi(n : ty("Nat")). ty("Nat")$ it ranges over all functions $NN -> NN$, the characteristic function of the halting problem included; for $sigma : ty("Type")_0$, over every set in $V_(kappa_0)$; for $sigma : ty("Prop")$, over $emptyset$ and ${bullet}$.
#why[the generic call binds each parameter to a fresh unknown, and an assignment says what those unknowns stand for. Ranging over every element of the model's sets, not only over values the machine can build, is what makes the conclusion a statement about the model's Π, which quantifies over every set function. A proof that ranged only over values the machine can build would not reach the model's statement (@sec-referees).]

#explainer[information orders][An _information order_ compares two descriptions of the same thing by how much they say. A description that leaves something open is _below_ one that fills it in, and nothing it does say may contradict the other. The least element says nothing at all. A step is _monotone_ if more information in gives more information out, or the same: a monotone step never turns "unknown" into a definite answer that more information would contradict.]

#thm([Definition], [meaning of a state], [
  Under an assignment $gamma$:
  - $sigma$ means $gamma(sigma)$; `⋆` means •; a constructor value means itself described: its declaration, the meanings of the parameters it records, its constructor and its fields' meanings, and, when it is whole and of its type, its element of the type's set; a type value means its set (@def-meaning), read at the meanings of what it embeds. A data type means its set tagged with its declaration and parameters (a _code_, as in Lean's tagged types). So a value is taken apart by reading it, never through its type: reading a field, a tag, or writing at a path consults neither the position's type nor the set, as an element of an inductive type in Lean's set-theoretic model is a tree that describes itself.
  - A sealed program #seal[$t$] means $deno(t, at: gamma)$, the meaning of running its program, its reference run (@def-refrun).
  - A closure means a function on its annotated domain: at each element of its parameter types' meanings, the meaning of its body with the parameters bound to that element. The function may be partial, taking an error value where the body goes wrong; no accepted derivation reaches such a point. A recursive closure, or a recursive global, has a meaning by _guarded_ recursion on the rank of its decreasing argument: a recursive call whose decreasing argument does not drop in rank means the error value. So every `fix` has a meaning, checked or not; passing [Rec] is what makes the guard never fire, which is part of membership.
  - A state with live borrows is read under a _future_ $phi$, which gives each live borrow's label ℓ its _final content_ $phi(ell)$, an element of the meaning of the borrow's content type. A loan of ℓ, at a place that lent its content, means $phi(ell)$: what the place will finally hold. A place holding $"borrow"_ell w$ means $w$'s meaning, its _current_ content, which is what a read through it sees. A sealed fill means its program, run with each hole read from $phi$. So a state means a function of a future, as a function returning a borrow means a backward function (@def-meaning). We write $deno(Omega, at: gamma)(phi)$ for the meaning of Ω under $gamma$ and $phi$.
  - An open dependent value means itself described, with no element of the set, since its fields need not lie in the set its telescope describes; a field read off it is typed by its content, as the checker types it, and [Repack] is where membership is restored.
  - `⊥` means _moved_. The model tracks moves: a place means a value or _moved_, and two places that are both moved mean the same. [Join] makes the moved places the same on every path, so this reading needs no record of what was moved. A constructor value with a moved field, or with a field outside its type, means itself described with no element of the set, never the error value. So each field is read off the meaning whatever the other fields hold: if a field outside its type made the whole value an error, reading one field of a pair would depend on, and so end, a borrow of the other field (found in Lean, @sec-spine).
  A _configuration_, a state together with a term still to run, means the reference run of the term from that state (@def-refrun).
]) <def-state-meaning>

*Why two readings.* A place that lent its content will be given back the borrow's final content, so it means that; a place holding the borrow can still read and write it, so it means what is there now. [End] connects the two: it fixes the borrow's final content at its holder's current content, and forgets the holder. The Lean encoding of the state (@sec-test-ho) found this shape necessary: one function from the final contents to the resolved contents cannot express a read through a borrow.

#why[every position of a state the checker builds now has a meaning that depends only on $gamma$ and on the final contents of the borrows still live. The fills with holes (with `Pick(n, x, y) := match n { Z ⇒ x, S _ ⇒ y }` at an unknown `n`, the hole of its returned borrow sits in the fills of both lent places) are ordinary functions of the hole; reading a place whose meaning depends on a live hole ends that borrow, which fixes the hole at the borrow's current content.]

*Why `⊥` means moved.* `⊥` could only mean _moved_ if every path moved the same places: otherwise a stuck block, which takes a place by move when some arm moves it, would say _moved_ where the arm an instance runs leaves the place whole or writes it. [Join] makes the direct run drop the same places at the end of the match, and reads them, at their grain, from syntax and declared types, so the generic call, every instance and the reference run agree. Equations can then decide on `⊥` (`⊥ = ⊥` is `True`, `⊥ = v` is `False`), and `Id` can relate sides that move the same places.

#thm([Definition], [less informative; ends], [
  A meaning $a$ is _less informative_ than $b$, $a lessinfo b$, when $a$ is $b$ with some of its parts replaced by _moved_: _moved_ is below everything, two constructor values with the same constructor are compared field by field, and anything else is below only itself. On _whole_ values, those with no moved part, the order is equality. An _end_ of a borrow ℓ in the meaning of a state fixes the future at ℓ to the current content of ℓ's holder and forgets the holder, which then means _moved_. An end loses only information: every reading of the state after it is, position by position, below a reading of the state before it. A step of the checker is _exact_ when the reference run makes the same change; since D90 every step is (below).
]) <def-lessinfo>

#why[since [Join], _moved_ is a definite value that equations decide on (@def-state-meaning), so this is not an order on what a program can observe. It was first the device that compared the checker's state with the reference run's. It no longer is: the simulation of @def-sim asks for equality. Comparing by the order admitted reference states that hold a value where the checker's holds _moved_ inside a tuple, and from such a state a match and `Id` compute differently. What remains of the order is that an end loses only information, which is proved in Lean.]

*Borrow ends are shared* (D90, D91). When a borrow ends is a decision read from syntax and declared types, as what is moved is ([Join], D84), so the generic call and every instance end the same borrows at the same points. A call whose declared result is a borrow lends that borrow every place its borrow arguments lend, whether it runs or closes off ([Lend]): a lent place holds a _fill_ over the borrow, a sealed program over its hole where the call closed off, a marked value $"lent"_k space v$ where it ran; the mark means $v$ and says only which borrow may point at the place. A match not in tail position, and `J`'s transported term, first access what the stuck block they would close off as captures, whichever arm runs ([Entry]); a stuck block does that before its arms are checked. A match on a value of a one-constructor type is never closed off, so it has no entry (D93): reading `x.0` does not end a borrow of `x.1`, as in Rust. So `Pick`'s end at an unknown `n` (reading `b` ends `r` though an instance at `n = 0` points into `a`) is the end every run makes, as in Rust. Before D90 the generic call ended such borrows early, an instance did not, and three accepted definitions had closed calls that failed (`EI`, `EJ`, `G1`; tests `ReturnedBorrows.StaticEnds`); the plan carried an _early end_ kind of step and a list of early-ended borrows in @def-sim, which are gone.

=== The theorem

#thm([Definition], [reference run], [
  The _reference run_ $refrun(t)$ of a term $t$, under an assignment and at a future $phi$, is a state transformer on _reference states_. A reference state has the checker's frames, bindings and temporaries, but holds at each position a meaning as a function of the future (@def-state-meaning) in place of a value; it records which borrow label each position holds, and it has a label counter. The run sends a reference state to the meaning of a result at $phi$, with its type, and a new reference state, or to the error value. It is defined by structural recursion on $t$, inside the model's recursion on program position and decreasing argument, with the machine's rules except four:
  - every call applies the callee's meaning (an earlier global's, a parameter's under the assignment, or a closure's) and never runs its body; for a callee returning a borrow, each lent place means its component of the backward function at the returned borrow's final content, and the returned borrow is lent every place its borrow arguments lend ([Lend]);
  - the reference state records where each live borrow is lent (each place, as a fill or as a loan), and before a place is read, borrowed, assigned, dropped or matched the run ends the live borrows lent at, over or under it (for a match, over it or at it), as the checker does, never asking what the place's meaning depends on; ending a borrow passes the places it was lent to the borrows of its content; a match not in tail position, on a type with several constructors, first accesses what the stuck block would capture ([Entry]), tail position following the checker's own recursion through a body's `let`s, sequences and arms; a closure's captured places are accessed before they are moved;
  - a match on data reads the tag of the scrutinee's meaning, which under an assignment is always a constructor, so nothing is stuck and nothing is split;
  - `Id`, `Eq` and `J` mean as in the model; a proof is not run at all and means `⋆`; any other statement (a type) runs on a copy that is thrown away, and its result names no borrow.
  A function's meaning is read off the reference run of its body in a fresh frame, with a cell holding each borrowed argument. That this meaning is compositional, a call changing only its result and its borrowed places, is the frame property (@sec-safety). On the slices, test 0 gave the pure part of this definition and test 2 the state, `Id` and the steps of a higher-order slice (@sec-tests); beyond them, Q2.
]) <def-refrun>

*What the reference run shares with the checker.* Everything else is the machine's rule, and that includes every _aligned_ decision (@sec-wf): the run must make the same choice as the checker wherever the choice changes the meaning. The mechanisation compared the run's clauses with the checker's evaluator. A first reading found six kinds of accepted program on which the run failed, so that those programs had no meaning; a case-by-case audit of 36 cases then found sixteen further differences (@sec-spine). Together they fixed what the run must share:
- _which terms are statements or proofs_ is asked of every term form, not only of calls: a proof-valued `let`, sequence or match is erased as a proof call is (`let h : True = (let y = x; refl); x` must leave `x` in place);
- _a call's head_, and the operand of `clone`, `peek` and `inplace`, is read in place when it is a place, not moved (`Twice (f : Π(n : Nat). Nat) (n : Nat) : Nat := f(f(n))`);
- _each parameter is bound as its declared type reads_: copied if the type is a copy type (`CopyParam (u : Unit) : Unit × Unit := (u, u)`), and with its full type, so that a function parameter can be called and a later parameter's type can call an earlier one;
- _each argument of a statement call_, and each field of a proof constructor, runs on its own copy unless its declared type is a borrow type (`let h : True = LemTwo(x, x); x`);
- _an assignment evaluates its right-hand side first_, then accesses the place (`let r = &y; y := clone(*r); y`), and an assignment through a borrow variable written whole ends nothing inside its content (D67);
- _an unwritten constructor parameter_ is read from the type the context expects, then from the fields' types, as the checker's constructor rule does;
- _the places a match drops at its join_ are read before the arm runs, and a match on a proof has no join; a borrow variable among them is dropped by ending its borrow, with nothing it lends accessed, so a reborrow of part of its content stays live. Accessing it first ended the reborrow, and on an accepted program that writes through the reborrow after the match and proves the written value by `refl`, the model lost the write and made the proved `Id` false;
- _the places already moved when a match starts_ are read at the grain of the value, as the checker reads them: every place with a moved part inside a variable's meaning, through a borrow variable's content, with a part still lent counting as not moved. Read as whole variables only, they made the join differ when a field was moved before a match one of whose arms refills it, and the checker then proved by `refl` an `Id` the model made false (`IdRefill`, accepted; reading the field after the match is rejected);
- _`Id`'s observation_ reads the positions each side started with, not the temporaries a side parked on the way;
- _[Obs-ref]'s written value ranges over the members of the borrowed type_, as the checker's fresh unknown of that type does. Ranging over every value made false an `Id` the checker rightly accepts: two sides that re-point `y : &Unit` at different places agree at every `Unit` written through `y`, and differ only when something outside `Unit` is written (`IdUnit`, accepted; the same at `Nat` is rejected);
- _types keep their structure where they are stored_: a function type's shape and a data type's parameters survive an annotation, a closure's captures, a parameter and a function's result type, and a binding that holds a type carries the structure of the type it holds, so that a `let`-bound alias `let T = Π(n : Nat). Nat; let g : T = f; g(n)` can be called through.
Each of these was a fault of the model, not of the checker.

*Where the run still differs from the checker.* These are open, each needing a redesign of part of the model rather than a changed clause:
- _a call's result_ carries no function shape, so a function returned by a call cannot be called through the result (`let a = Adder(n); a(Z)`); it does carry its data type's declaration and parameters, read off the codomain's code;
- _a function type computed by a call_ has no shape either. With `Fn(A) := Π(x : A). A`, a parameter `f : Fn(Nat)` called as `f(n)` is accepted: the checker stores the Π-type the call computed, and calls through it; the reference run's binding has the call's result as its type, with no shape, and the call through `f` fails. This is the ordinary predicate idiom (`P : Pred(Nat)` with `Pred(A) := Π(x : A). Prop`, then `P(n)`);
- _a field read_ loses the same structure for a function stored in a field (a field holding a constructor value is typed by its content);
- _a proof has no type in the run_, which does not run it. So a constructor whose type parameter only a proof field's type determines fails in the run and is accepted by the checker: `let b = MkPBox(h); n` with `PBox(A : Prop) := MkPBox(a : A)`;
- _an open dependent value_ is read by its content: its meaning describes itself and its fields are typed by what they hold, as in the checker; [Open] and [Repack] are not yet stated against the run (Q3);
- _`clone` or `peek` of a partly moved place_: the checker reads the place as it is, and the run fails. No accepted program is known that shows it.
On programs that use a call's result, a computed function type or a field as a function or as the expected type of a bare constructor, and on a constructor whose parameter a proof field determines, the reference run fails where the checker succeeds. So until the redesign of a call's result lands, @thm-mp as mechanised is _false_ on such programs: not because the checker is wrong, but because the model has no meaning for what the checker computed. One redesign closes the first two (a function's type gives the structure of its result as a function of its arguments; designed in the design note, D22, not built); the field read and the proof's type need designs of their own. Three smaller differences: a closure's captured type alias and an assignment to a type variable keep the old structure; the search for an unwritten constructor parameter does not look two levels deep (`List(Pair(A, B))`); and `J` at convertible endpoints drops at a join in the checker and not in the run. The label counter after a private copy, which the checker keeps and the run restores, is no longer a difference: the relation of @def-sim absorbs it, and that is proved (@sec-spine). The run's side of the comparison was read off its clauses; it is kernel-checked only where a named Lean lemma says so. One kernel-checked run per term form, on a small accepted program, would catch a wrong clause before a step proof does, and is not yet done.

#thm([Definition], [simulation], [
  Let Ω be a checker state, $gamma$ an assignment of it, $S$ a reference state, $phi$ the future the reference run is at, and $pi$ a _renaming_: a function from the checker's borrow labels to the reference's. $S$ _simulates_ Ω under $pi$ at $phi$ when four things hold.
  - _Skeleton._ The two have the same frames, and each frame has the same bindings in the same order, with types of the same meaning, the same function shape (the parameters and result the reference run applies a function of that type with) and, for a data type, the same parameters, and with the same declared readings. A checker binding that holds a type is read with the structure of the type it holds. Temporaries are not part of the skeleton.
  - _Labels._ Every label that occurs in Ω, as a loan or a borrow somewhere inside a value of Ω, is renamed by $pi$ to a label $S$ has issued, so the next label each side issues is new on both.
  - _Holders and meanings._ For every future $psi$ that agrees with $phi$ off the labels live in $S$, reading Ω under $gamma$ at the future $psi compose pi$: each binding of Ω means the same as the same binding of $S$ at $psi$; a binding holds the borrow ℓ in Ω exactly when it holds $pi(ell)$ in $S$; a borrow ℓ held by a temporary of Ω (a value _in flight_, computed and not yet bound) is held under the label $pi(ell)$ by some temporary of the same frame of $S$, with the same content, and every borrow a temporary of $S$ holds is so matched. $pi$ is one to one on the live labels.
  - _Lending._ Each live borrow ℓ of Ω is lent at the same places in $S$ under $pi(ell)$, each as a fill or as a loan alike.
]) <def-sim>

A checker state, read as a reference state, simulates itself, with the identity renaming. Steps stated from _any_ simulating state compose: if the reference run of $t_1$ from every state simulating Ω reaches one simulating $Omega_1$, and that of $t_2$ from every state simulating $Omega_1$ reaches one simulating $Omega_2$, then the run of $t_1$ followed by $t_2$ from every state simulating Ω reaches one simulating $Omega_2$. And the relation survives a private copy: when the checker runs a statement on a copy and restores its state, keeping the label counter and the records the copy made, the same reference state simulates the restored state under the same renaming. All three are proved in Lean (`Rel.start`, `sim_seq` and `Rel.private_copy`, @sec-spine).

*Why a relation, and why these parts.* The first form of this plan had no relation. The reference ran from the checker's own state, read as a reference state, and the checker's next state was to be less informative than the reference's: for every future of the one a future of the other at which every position is below. Stating that in Lean showed it to be false, and to be no use even where true. Each part of the relation answers one fault.
- _It did not compose._ After one step the reference is in its own state, not in the checker's next state read as a reference state, and the next step's statement said nothing about a run from there. So a step is stated from any simulating state.
- _It was false once a borrow had been held._ On the accepted `let r = &y; ()` the reference run parks the borrow in a temporary until it is bound, and the temporary stays behind, meaning _moved_; the checker carries the borrow in flight and has no temporary there. A missing position means the error value, which is not below _moved_. So temporaries are compared by the label they hold, not by position.
- _Meanings do not say where a variable is._ Two states with the same meanings and different numbers of bindings resolve the same variable to different positions. So the skeleton is part of the relation.
- _A binding's type is more than its meaning._ The reference run calls a function through the shape its type carries. A first skeleton compared only the meaning, and so admitted a reference state whose bindings had lost their shapes, from which every call through a variable fails; the step for evaluation, stated from any simulating state, was then false of every accepted call through a variable. So the skeleton compares the shape. Whether the shape the checker's stored type reads as is the shape the reference's binding got from its own run is a separate question, and it is false as a blanket claim: the run drops the structure of a call's result, a field and a proof (@def-refrun).
- _The two runs do not issue the same labels, and cannot be made to._ The checker runs a proof on a private copy and keeps its label counter afterwards, so a proof that borrows, such as `lemma(&x)`, uses up labels; the reference run does not run a proof at all, and could fail if it did. A first version of the relation asked for equal counters and was false after any such proof. So labels are compared through a renaming, which each step extends at the labels it issues.
- _Only the labels that occur can be renamed._ A second version asked the renaming of every label the checker had issued, and was false after any statement that borrows: after the type `Eq(Nat, (let r = &y; Z), Z)`, checked from a state with no labels, the checker has issued one label and the reference none, and no renaming sends a label issued to a label issued. A label issued inside a private copy occurs nowhere after it (by (WF11), @sec-wf), so the renaming is asked only of the labels that occur.
- _An inert loan is read at different futures._ A binding that holds a sealed program's hole means the future's value at that label in the checker's state, and the constant the run read at its own future in the reference state. So the futures compared are those that agree with the run's off the live labels.
- _The two are equal._ A third version compared each binding by the order of @def-lessinfo. That admitted a reference state holding a value where the checker's holds _moved_ inside a tuple. From such a state the places already moved when a match starts differ, so the join differs, and `Id`, which compares every position after each side, is true for the checker and false for the reference. So the relation asks for equality.
Of the order of @def-lessinfo, nothing is left in the relation: the free choice of a second future, the comparison of temporaries by position and the comparison of values by order are gone.

*Lending, in place of a witness.* Until D90 the checker ended some borrows early and the reference ended a borrow when what a place's meaning depended on changed. The relation then carried the list of early-ended borrows and a _loan witness_ pinning the reference state where an early-ended label had another value, since its [Access] read every future: without it a reference position meaning "the content of $k$ when $l$ is the error value, and $c$ otherwise" agreed with the checker's constant once $l$ was ended and still depended on $k$ (checked in Lean, with an injective backward function making such conditional dependence reachable). With borrow ends shared, the reference's [Access] reads where each borrow is lent, which the relation makes the checker's, and every read the reference run makes of its state is at a future the relation compares (the closed future after an access, the holder at an end). So the relation determines the run, and the witness and the list went (docs/31 D29). That after an access the content reads no live borrow follows at related states from the equality of meanings and from a value's meaning at a path reading the future only at the loans, marks and holes at comparable paths; it fails at an open dependent value if a later field's type is read from an earlier lent field, so the reference reads a field's type with the earlier fields at their current contents, as the checker's does. Two clauses of well-formedness go with lending: under a fill only what ending it reaches ((WF12)), and no borrow is lent inside its own holder's content ((WF3) on where borrows are lent). Both are read off the checker's D90, not proved: no lemma yet shows that a checker step keeps them, and until one does they are assumptions of the steps that use them. The reference's lending has been checked against the checker's on the checker's own states for two of the attacks on it (a reborrow that outlives its borrow, and a borrow-returning call at a known argument): the sites and the holders agree after each step, and reading a lender ends the borrow on both sides (docs/31 `Regress/StaticLend`).

#why[the reference run is the denotation of the term as a state transformer, not a second machine run: it never runs a callee's body, it applies the callee's meaning, which at a function parameter ranges over every set function. It is the standard each step of the checker is measured against.]

#thm([Theorem], [meaning preservation, membership and well-formedness], [
  Let $cal(P) = d_1 dots d_m$ be accepted and hole-free, and let the checker, while checking $d_k$, run a term $t$ from a state Ω satisfying (b) and (c). For every assignment $gamma$ of Ω, following at each split the arm whose constructor $gamma$ selects, the run reaches a value $v$ and a state Ω', and:
  - (a) _Simulated by the reference run._ For every future $phi$, every renaming $pi$ and every reference state $S$ that simulates Ω under $pi$ at $phi$ (@def-sim), there are an assignment $gamma'$ of Ω', extending $gamma$ to the unknowns the run created and satisfying its path condition, and a renaming $pi'$, agreeing with $pi$ on the labels Ω had issued, such that: $refrun(t)$ from $S$ at $phi$, under $gamma'$, is not the error value; the meaning of $v$ under $gamma'$, read at the future $phi compose pi'$, is its result; and the state it reaches simulates Ω' under $pi'$ at $phi$. It is proved one rule at a time, each step exact.
  - (b) _Membership._ Every value stored in Ω', at a binding, a temporary or in Δ, has its meaning in the meaning of its stored type under every typed future: $deno(v, at: gamma')(phi) in deno(T, at: gamma')$ for every future $phi$ giving each live borrow a final content of its type. (Since a type means only its good elements, this includes the injectivity of every backward function, $R$ of @def-inj.) A value with parts moved out is a member when it is a member with those parts moved: it means _moved_, or it lies below a whole member of the type with the same constructor, each of its fields being that member's field or a field with parts moved out in turn. A match binds no variables, so `match xs { Cons(h, t) => t }` leaves `xs` partly moved; with membership read as "below some whole member in the order of @def-lessinfo", which compares a tuple only with a tuple, no partly moved value was a member of anything, and (b) failed after every move of a field (found in Lean).
  - (c) _Well-formedness._ Ω' is well formed (@sec-wf).
  (a), (b) and (c) are proved together, by induction on the checker's derivation for $d_k$, with the hypothesis taken at every assignment and every future, inside an outer induction on $d_k$'s position in $cal(P)$ and the size of its decreasing argument under the assignment.
]) <thm-mp>

#why[(a) says the checker computes what the meaning of the term computes, and that this stays so however many steps are chained; (b) says it never stores a value in a slot its type does not describe, whatever the live borrows write there later; (c) says the borrow discipline that gives the state a meaning still holds. None of the three suffices alone, as test 1 showed: of the five known proofs of `False`, part (a) alone catches one (@sec-tests).]

*The induction, as mechanised.* The checker's functions call one another with a step budget that drops at each call, so the Lean proof is an induction on that budget: each step lemma is a statement indexed by it, one record holds them all, and each is proved at a budget one larger from the record at the budget below (@sec-spine). No step lemma can be proved alone, since ending a borrow substitutes, substitution re-runs sealed programs, and a re-run evaluates. The hypothesis is taken at every assignment and every future, not only those the step starts with, because a step reads its state at other futures (an end, membership) and hands on an extended assignment. For the same reason the code in a state (a sealed program, a closure, a function type's codomain) is read by the reference run at whatever assignment and future it is asked at; an earlier Lean version fixed both once for the whole induction, and then read every sealed program at the wrong future.

*The run context.* The reference run reads more than the state: the inductive declarations, a set-theoretic model of them, the types of the unknowns, the meanings of the globals, and the aligned decisions. A step lemma holds only for a context that is the checker state's own, and the Lean statements assume so, in one hypothesis: the context's declarations are the state's; its data model covers each of them; it gives each unknown of the state its stored type; and each global means its stored value, read at its stored type, holds no borrow, and is a member of that type; its decisions are the checker's at the state: wherever the checker's own decision procedure answers, the context's decision gives the same answer (@sec-wf); and the checker's typing of places holds in the state: at every place in scope, by its declared reading, an unknown at a place declared an inductive has that inductive's type, such a place holds a constructor value of it or a stand-in (`⊥`, `⋆`, an unknown, a sealed program, a loan, a mark), a place declared a borrow type holds a borrow, `⊥`, a loan or a mark over one, every constructor value has its constructor's fields, and every loan and mark is of a live borrow. This typing is syntactic, and membership does not give it (the model reads `&A` as `A`); it is read off the checker and checked at every place in scope of every state the suite reaches. The clause on globals is the outer induction's hypothesis, which the assembly over the program's definitions discharges. Every step assumes the hypothesis and concludes it of the state it reaches, the decisions' and the typing's clauses included. Without the hypothesis every step lemma is false: a constant means whatever the context says, so a context whose globals are not the program's refutes the lemma for evaluation (checked in Lean). A step that creates unknowns hands on an extended context with its extended assignment; that what was established under the old one carries over is a locality lemma, proved (@sec-spine).

*For a split, every assignment has an arm.* An assignment gives the scrutinee $sigma$ an element of $deno(ty("D")(overline(a)), at: gamma)$, which is a disjoint union of its constructors' field tuples (@def-meaning). So exactly one arm's constructor matches, and $gamma$ extends to that arm's fresh field unknowns by the element's fields; the arm's refinement $sigma := ty("C")_i (overline(sigma))$ is then a substitution that changes no meaning. A split on a sealed program $n$ first generalises it to a fresh $sigma$ with the path condition $gamma(sigma) = deno(n, at: gamma)$, which $gamma$ satisfies by taking that value. So the case tree covers every assignment, and no step needs a machine value with a head constructor, which a non-computable witness does not have (@sec-referees).

*Where (a) needs (b).* Most steps keep meaning by computation alone. Two kinds replace a value by a canonical one: a value whose declared type is a proposition becomes `⋆`, and a value whose declared type reads as `Unit`, or as a one-constructor type (η), becomes `()` or that constructor. Those keep meaning only if the value lies in its type's meaning: $deno(v, at: gamma) in deno(P, at: gamma) subset.eq {bullet}$, or in the one-element set $deno(ty("Unit"))$. For a stuck call that becomes `()` at a declared `Unit`, that is $deno(f, at: gamma)(overline(a)) in deno(ty("Unit"))$, membership of $f$'s meaning in its Π-type. That comes from the outer induction for an earlier global; from the outer induction at a smaller argument for a recursive call, since [Rec] made its decreasing argument a strict part of the entry value, and the strict-part relation on a least fixed point is well founded (@sec-sets); from (b) at an earlier step for a closure built earlier in the run; and from $gamma$ itself for a function parameter.

*Where (b) and (c) are earned.* (b) and (c) are not consequences of (a); they are established at particular steps, and the checker's _checks_ are where. Membership of a value that leaves an open dependent value is established by [Repack] at every whole-again point, under every future of the holes still lent from it; no stored type depends on a hole, since the one type computed over a lent field, an open value's, quantifies over the hole (D64 amended, D73; test 2), and the check of a lent part is sound as implemented (@sec-test-census); membership of a block's or a `J`'s sealed result by the return check at the end of each arm; membership of a recursive function, that its guard never fires, by [Rec]; membership of a function returning a borrow, that its backward function is injective, by an invariant that every hole is placed injectively in the state, kept by [Access], D67, D65 and (C8) (test 2); and membership of a returned borrow's forward value by the requirement that a returned borrow be whole. A check that fails produces no derivation, so only its accepting direction carries an obligation, and that obligation is exactly the part of (b) or (c) the check is there to establish.

*Why this is ordinary induction.* Every appeal is to an earlier step of the same derivation or to the outer hypothesis. There is no separate typing invariant to establish first: (b) is that invariant, proved step by step alongside (a). Nor can a step meet code that was never checked: a sealed call of an unchecked global could re-run to anything (an unchecked `F (n : Nat) : Nat := Nat` re-runs to a type at a refined argument), but while $d_k$ is checked every global is $d_1, dots, d_(k-1)$, checked and covered by the outer induction, or $d_k$ itself at a smaller argument.

#thm([Corollary], [an accepted definition means an element of its type], [
  Under @thm-mp and @lem-conv, for every assignment $gamma$ of $d_k$'s generic state, the meaning of $d_k$'s body (its result and the final contents of its borrowed cells) lies in $deno(G, at: gamma)$, its goal. So $deno(d_k)$, the function sending each choice of arguments to that meaning, lies in the meaning of $d_k$'s declared Π-type.
]) <cor-mean>
#proof[Follow the case tree from the root under $gamma$, using (a) at each step: every assignment reaches one leaf $(Omega', v, A)$, with an extended assignment $gamma'$. By (b), $deno(v, at: gamma') in deno(A, at: gamma')$. [Def] checked $A equiv G'$, the goal refined along the path, so by @lem-conv $deno(A, at: gamma') = deno(G', at: gamma')$, which is $deno(G, at: gamma)$ because the path's refinements are substitutions and its records are $gamma'$'s path condition. By (a), started from the generic state read as a reference state, which simulates itself (@def-sim), the reference run of the body does not fail, and its result, which is $d_k$'s meaning at the arguments $gamma$ chooses, is the leaf's value; the borrowed cells are bindings, which the relation compares position by position, and they mean the same. The result and the borrowed cells' final contents are whole at a leaf ([Return]).]

#thm([Corollary], [consistency], [
  Under @thm-mp, @lem-conv and @lem-model, @target holds.
]) <cor-cons-route>
#proof[A parameterless definition $c$ is checked from the empty state ([Const]), which has exactly one assignment, the empty one. By @cor-mean, $deno(c) in deno(T)$ for the value $T$ of its type. If $T$ computed to `False`, then by @lem-conv $deno(T) = deno(ty("False")) = emptyset$, which has no element.]

=== What the checker must satisfy <sec-wf>

The theorem asks four things of the checker. None asks that a run make progress or terminate; consistency needs neither.

*Well-formedness, part (c).* Backward functions mean the right thing only under the borrow discipline, so @def-state-meaning is defined only for states satisfying (WF1)–(WF6) of @sec-states (each label has at most one borrow; loans only where closing off allows; no cycles among borrows; closures capture no borrow; arguments exclusive at call points; bindings hold values of their types) and
- (WF7) `&` is applied only to types in `Type₀`, so that a borrow means forward and backward functions on data.
[Rec] is not among them: guarded recursion gives every `fix` a meaning, checked or not (test 0), and passing [Rec] is what makes the guard never fire, which is membership.
The mechanisation added eight clauses, each read off the checker and each needed for a state to have the meaning @def-state-meaning gives it, or for the drop at a match's join:
- (WF8) `&` is applied to no borrow type and never sits inside a data type or an equation: no borrow type is a parameter of an inductive type or of a constructor value, or the type or a side of `Eq`, wherever the type sits (in a value, a stored type, an unknown's type, the goal, or code's recorded readings). So no state holds `Eq(Type₀, &A, A)`, which the model's reading of `&A` as $A$ would make true. As first stated (`&` only at the top of a stored type, and nowhere inside a value) the sweep of the checker's states found it false at a borrow type in flight and at a closed-off stuck block, whose function keeps `&A` as a captured place's declared reading; both have `&` at the top of a type, which the rules allow;
- (WF9) a loan outside a sealed program sits at a position with a stored type, because the content [End] writes back there is read at that type;
- (WF10) every constructor value in the state records its type's parameters (D88, D89), so that a value's meaning does not depend on the type its context expects;
- (WF11) types and records hold no loan: no stored or deferred type of a binding, no type of an unknown, not the goal, and no generalisation or η-record holds a loan anywhere, a sealed program's holes included;
- (WF12) under a live fill only borrows filled there: at a position holding a live borrow's fill, every live borrow lent at the fill's place or inside it is also filled at that place, so an access that passes the fill on its way to one field ends the same borrows as an access of the filled place whole. Two borrows can be filled at one place: when a returned borrow ends, its fill passes to every borrow its content was lent onward to, and since D93 two disjoint parts of a returned borrow's place can be lent onward together. A stronger form (no other borrow lent at or inside a fill) held on every state the checker reached until D93 made that program accepted;
- (WF14) no live borrow is lent strictly above a moved part, so where the checker's dropped place is already moved, the reference's access of the lent prefix does nothing either;
- (WF15) no declared type a closure or a function type records holds a loan, so a loan the checker's scans do not see is nowhere;
- (WF16) every sealed program ends, on every path, in something whose meaning does not depend on the type it is read at, so writing `⊥` beside one leaves its meaning alone.
- The model reads a data value by its own description, never by its type or its set: carriers do not determine declarations (two declarations may have equal carriers, which Lean can neither prove nor refute), and the mechanisation's first reading, which decoded by carrier, took the wrong arm of a match under a model making two declarations' carriers equal (docs/31 D31); its second, which decoded by the position's type, did not find a field's own value after an earlier field was moved or rewritten, on the accepted programs `JK` and `OWK` (docs/31 `Regress/PartialMoveDep`, `Regress/OpenWriteDep`). Reading the value itself (docs/31 D32) needs no clause.
(WF11) is what three things rest on. A type and a record are read at a fixed future, which is the reading at the run's future only for a value with no loan. [End] substitutes a loan in the values of Ω alone, so a loan in a type or a record would outlive its borrow. And what survives a private copy beside the restored values is exactly the records and the types of the unknowns created inside it, so with (WF11) no label issued inside a copy occurs after it, which is what lets the renaming of @def-sim absorb the label counters' difference. It is read off the checker (a sealed program is generalised only in typed mode, after [Access] has ended every live loan inside it, and in a typed run every loan is live), and no accepted program is known that breaks it; that every step preserves it is not yet proved. (WF12) is likewise read off the checker (a returned borrow's mark wraps the whole lent place; when a borrow ends its mark passes, at the same place, to every borrow its content was lent onward to; reaching below a mark ends its borrow first; lending a place whole ends the borrows of its parts), and that every step preserves it is not yet proved.
*The sweep.* The checker can record the states it reaches (`Config.recordStates`), and `lake exe sweep` evaluates exact Boolean copies of the clauses that need no meaning ((WF1)–(WF4), (WF6), (WF8)–(WF12), and "no live fill above a moved part", which the drop at the join uses) on every recorded state: 69,949 states over the suite's 2,162 declarations, and 538 states of adversarial programs written to make the clauses' premises arise. Two deliberately false clauses guard against a vacuous run. It found three clauses wrong as first stated, none a checker fault: (WF8) as above; (WF1), violated inside the private copy that evaluates a call's parameter types, which held a borrow argument twice (fixed in the checker, D92: the copy takes it from its holder; the reference binds earlier arguments by meaning and holds no label twice); and "no live fill above a moved or proof part", false for proof parts (a place lent whole keeps its proof field under the mark), now stated for moved parts. A proof part is dropped at the join only if it is not of a copy type, so only as a data field of a proof; whether that can happen under a live fill is untested, and if it can, the generic call and an instance end different borrows. Rerun with the restated clauses (and (WF6)'s counter equal to the number of typed unknowns), every clause holds on every recorded state of the suite and the probes, and both planted clauses are violated. A live fill occurs in 1,825 suite states and 224 probe states. In the probes, since D93, another live borrow is lent at a live fill's position in 18 states and at its place in 3, where both borrows are filled there; this broke the stronger form of (WF12), which was restated to the one above, and holds on every state. (WF6) was later restated to bound every unknown the state names (@sec-spine); rerun, it holds on every state. A second probe ends every live borrow from every recorded state with the checker's own [End] and compares the unknowns and records before and after; it found the unknowns [Repack] keeps (@sec-spine).
The preservation of well-formedness is not yet proved; test 2 assumed it, and found that the preservation of (WF2) needs (WF3). In the mechanisation it is part of what the step lemmas conclude, with separate statements for a borrow, an end, an assignment, closing off and a capture. (WF5), loan-free arguments at a call, is not only a runtime matter: with a live loan in an argument, a read now would depend on a write made later, so the meaning of a state needs it.

*Borrow ends are shared, step by step.* Since D90 a step of the checker and the reference run's matching step end the same borrows: both end, at an access, the borrows lent at, over or under the place, read from where each is lent, which the relation makes the same (@def-sim). Each leaf step (a read, a borrow, an assignment, the constructor a match reads, a call) is to be proved from any reference state that simulates the checker's: the ends agree by the lending clause, and what the leaf then reads is the same because the content reads no live borrow at a related state. The same holds of every step that _computes_ on a state (`Eq` and `J` computing, a match choosing an arm, [Close] choosing its row, conversion, [Rewrite]'s search): they read the same values. `⊥` needs no argument: by [Join] it means _moved_ on every path. The generalisation records, which compare sealed programs by text, are safe: `⊥` reaches a data-valued sealed program only inside an erased statement.

Two leaves were judged the likeliest to fail in the simulation form, and were attacked first, from a simulating state; neither is proved yet. _The drop at the join_ reads which places are moved. Before D90 a holder the checker had ended early was _moved_ in its state and not in the reference's, and that difference was read as harmless; it was not: the first attempt at the drop's proof found that an early end made the generic call's drop skip a move an instance made (`EJ`), which led to D90. The attack found three faults elsewhere, all fixed: partly moved values were members of nothing (@thm-mp, (b)); the reference read the places already moved at the grain of whole variables (@def-refrun); and the relation compared by order where it needed equality (@def-sim). _`Id`'s observation_ reads every position a side started with, temporaries included. Read against the checker, it agrees: a borrow holder and a temporary are _moved_ after every borrow ends, on both sides, and the footprint covers the rest, given that the two sides leave the same type-state. The attack found one fault of the model, fixed ([Obs-ref]'s written value, @def-refrun), and three of the statements, fixed: the statements for `Id` and `Eq` were satisfied by any wrong witness, through a misplaced bracket; and footprint coverage lacked the hypothesis that the two sides move the same places, without which it is false, since the footprint lists what the sides write and borrow, not what they move. One lemma in place of the leaf-by-leaf argument, that the reference run is monotone in its state, is not available: for the order of @def-lessinfo it is false (two states can be related through a future that no end would fix; the lower reads `5` where the upper's holder holds `7`, and a read then gives `5` against `7`).

*Decisions, by kind.* A decision need not come out the same on the generic call and at every instance. It must fall into one of four kinds, and the census of @sec-tests sorted every one:
- _same_: every outcome keeps the meaning, possibly given (b). η at `Unit` and at one-constructor types (the type has one element); a proof's value being `⋆`; `Eq` and `J` computing; [Split], records and re-normalisation.
- _aligned_: the outcomes differ in meaning, so the model must make the same choice, and it can because the decision reads only syntax and declared types, which the model's meaning of code reads the same way. Whether a term is erased (run in place, or on a private copy that is thrown away, discarding writes through borrows); a function type's class; whether a statement argument runs in place; [Close]'s row; copy or move (D80); the places a match moves at its join and their grain ([Join], D84); and the type-state of an `Id` and [Obs-ref]'s set (D84). A binding's class is one, read from syntax since D77. So are the choices the reference run was found to need (@def-refrun): that a call's head is read in place, how a parameter's declared type is read, and which argument of a statement call runs on its own copy. The mechanisation gives the reference run these decisions as functions it is handed; that they are the checker's is a clause of the run context's hypothesis (@def-refrun), and the statements per decision are proved from it. Since D90 the captures of the stuck block a match would close off ([Entry]) are a decision too. The checker computes them partly from values (where a moved place is re-rooted, whether a variable holds a borrow, whether a capture is a proof, how a capture splits into fields); the reference reads each from declared types instead, and that the two agree on the checker's states is D90's first obligation, stated, checked on one program, and not proved.
- _less informative_: none since D90. Borrow ends were of this kind (a stuck block's captures by borrow and a stuck call's fills over-approximate a run's loans), and are now aligned: the generic call and every instance lend the same places and end the same borrows ([Lend], [Entry]).
- _check_: only accepts or rejects, so only the accepting direction needs an argument (above). Some checks establish (b), some (c), and some protect only runtime safety, which consistency does not need but type safety (@target-safe) does: the requirement that a borrow be whole when it is passed to a call that closes off. The no-ending clause of [Id-typestate] is a check of this kind, judged on the run that checks the program (census site 28, class (c)): it only rejects, so it costs @target nothing. (That a returned borrow be whole is membership: a returned borrow holding `⊥` has a forward value with no meaning.)

*Five lemmas behind "same".* The decisions of kind _same_ rest not on one argument per site but on five lemmas: membership, part (b); the soundness of the function that reads a value's own type (`valType?`: the meaning of a value lies in the meaning of the type it reads, under every assignment), used where a type is read off an embedded value; _footprint coverage_, that the places `Id` observes include every place either side may write, under every assignment, with the extra owners at a call site handled by the injectivity of contexts (a context holding a loan is injective in the loan's final value, which test 2's invariant that holes are placed injectively provides); conversion soundness (@lem-conv); and well-formedness, part (c).

=== The lemmas each step uses

The step lemmas, one per checker rule, are where the principles of @sec-principles are used, read as statements about meaning:
- worlds (X1)–(X4): leaving an arm drops every fact that depends on the arm's path condition, so the assignment that held in the arm's world is never asked to hold elsewhere; keeping such a fact is how `EtaBoom` proved `False` (@sec-risks);
- closing off (C1)–(C9): [Close] is exact by definition, because a sealed program means its program, and (C1) is what makes the text rebuild the call;
- locality (L1)–(L7), read as the compositionality of the meaning of code: a call's meaning changes only its result and its borrowed places (the frame property);
- observation (O1)–(O5): `Id`'s computation to a conjunction over the footprint is exact by footprint coverage;
- erasure and captures (E1)–(E5), (K1)–(K3): erased runs keep meaning because their copies are discarded, and captures move exactly [Join]'s set, while captures by borrow can lead to ends (@def-lessinfo);
- wholeness (W1)–(W2): every point where a value passes to code relying on its type is a point where (b) is established for it;
- recursion (R1)–(R6): membership of recursive functions (the guard never fires) and the outer induction.

=== From meanings to type safety <sec-safety>

Type safety (@target-safe) reuses the route. The checker runs code in two modes: _typed_, when it checks a definition's text, and _untyped_, when it runs a callee's body or re-runs a sealed program. The step lemmas of @thm-mp speak of both, and a closed run is an untyped run from a state with no unknowns, so they were expected to cover concrete runs as they stand. The mechanisation found a qualification. An untyped run of _arbitrary_ text is not simulated: the checker's untyped run can succeed on text that has no meaning (an unannotated `let x = Nil` yields a constructor with no parameters). The step lemma for untyped runs must assume that the code is checked code, in the form a proof can use: the reference run of that code from the simulating state does not fail. Membership of the callee's meaning gives that for a callee's body, and membership with the substitution lemma for a re-run. For a closed call of an accepted function, at arguments built by accepted code, it is membership of the function's meaning in its type (@cor-mean). So the reuse is expected to stand in this narrower form; the restated lemma is open (@sec-order), and until it is proved the claim that closed runs are covered rests on it. And the reference run of an accepted definition never goes wrong (@thm-mp, part (a)). What remains is to connect the erased run with the reference run. Test 3 (@sec-test-safety) worked this out on a slice:
- (a) _Error reflection splits by kind of error._ The machine's 140 error sites fall into four families.
  - _Value errors_ (11 sites: no such place, not a function, a value no arm matches) reflect to the reference run: at a closed state a value means itself, so the machine's test of its shape fails exactly where the reference run's does. Following `*` from a value that is not a borrow is the exception, since a borrow means its content; it is excluded by typing, which the runtime invariant must therefore include.
  - _Borrow errors_ (18 sites: moved out, a borrow ending or returned partly moved, a dying value borrowed by one in flight) also reflect to the reference run, using the injectivity of backward functions, except at _one-element types_ (`Unit`, `CellsEnd`). There the machine can hold a loan that no meaning depends on: in `UseDirect (u : Unit) : Unit := (let r = &u; let z = u; *r := (); ())` the machine ends `r` when it reads `u` and then errs at `*r := ()`, while the reference run, whose `u` means `()` in every future, ends nothing. Those errors reflect instead to the checker's derivation, by a lemma about borrow structure only: at corresponding points of the closed run and the derivation, the same places are moved (exactly, by [Join]), every borrow the run has ended the derivation has ended, and every live loan of the run has a corresponding loan or hole in the derivation. The lemma is sketched by cases, with no counterexample; the checker rejects `UseDirect` for exactly this reason.
  - _Static and typing errors_ (93 sites) are unreachable: the erased run does no typing and closes nothing off, and every check on syntax passed when the definition was checked. _Internal assertions_ (18) are invariants.
  #s-paper; checked in Lean operation by operation, not yet over a whole run.
- (b) _Runtime `J` is its transported term_ ([J-run], D85). The checker computes `J` only at convertible endpoints, and a closed run can reach a `J` whose endpoints have equal meanings but are not convertible: with `f = λ(n : Nat) : Nat => n` and `g = λ(n : Nat) : Nat => match n { Z => Z, S m => S(m) }`, both the identity on $NN$, a cast along a true `Eq(Π(n : Nat). Nat, f, g)` is stuck. In the erased runtime, $ty("J")(A, a, b, P, h, t)$ evaluates to $t$ unconditionally: this is what erasure means, since the endpoints, motive and proof are statements the erased program does not have; it is sound given membership, since true proof parameters make the endpoints' meanings equal (Lean: `castRT_safe`); and it reads nothing. The checker's rule would get stuck there, on two encodings of one function (Lean: `cast_fn_counterexample`). The rule is [J-run] (D85, on main): the runtime's entry point, `runCall` in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/Runtime.lean`, runs a closed call of an accepted global in _runtime mode_, the only mode in which [J-run] fires, and no code the checker runs sets that mode.
  #why[the checker must keep its own rule, everywhere, its untyped runs included: those run callee bodies and sealed programs' re-runs at unknowns and under hypotheses that may be false, where a stuck `J` is what keeps an equation from being reflected into conversion. With runtime mode switched on inside the checker, 4 of the suite's 1,942 verdicts change, among them the rejected `JT` and `CastFnRuns` becoming accepted.]
  Consistency needs nothing here: truth is semantic, and a stuck `J` means transport, the identity at endpoints with equal meanings, which membership of the equation's proof guarantees. One caveat: D85's runtime runs statements on private copies rather than skipping them; by the erasure argument, (c) below, the two are equivalent.
- (c) _Erasure holds by construction._ A statement's run leaves no trace (it runs on a discarded copy); no runtime step reads a statement's value except as `⋆` (proofs by declared sort, D55; matches on proofs by declared type, D45, with (I7); classes by syntax, D54, D77; no type values in data, (I6)); and calls through function values erase alike. Runtime `J` is its one extra clause. #s-paper.
- (d) _Termination._ Recursion by name terminates: [Rec] makes each recursive call's argument a component of the entry value, so of smaller rank, and guarded recursion's guard never fires. Calls through function values are not covered by that measure: a closure passed in, or stored in data, can be called from anywhere, and a function's meaning (its graph) bounds no number of steps. They need a _realizability_ relation in the style of Tait and Girard, between runtime values and meanings, defined by the model's recursion, at a type variable over pairs of a set and a relation; it is predicative, since proofs are erased. Not yet built: this is Q4. That is why @target-safe asks for arguments built by accepted code.
- (e) _Well-formedness at runtime_ is the checking-time invariant with no inert loans, shared with consistency (@sec-wf). The Lean check proves (WF2), (WF3), and that no borrow sits inside a borrow's content, preserved by [End], [Access], [Drop] and a call; not yet a borrow, assignment or move at a field path, nor (WF1). The injectivity of backward functions is needed for type safety as well as consistency: the reflection of [Drop] rests on it.

*Open lemmas for @target-safe:* the borrow-structure lemma, sketched; realizability, for termination through function values; typing in the runtime invariant; error reflection over a whole run, not per operation; and well-formedness at field paths, with (WF1).

*The frame property is a lemma, not a target.* Both targets use it: for consistency it is the compositionality of the meaning of code (a call's meaning changes only its result and its borrowed places), and for type safety the fact that a call's effect at runtime is its isolated run, plugged back into the caller.

*Adequacy* in the narrow sense is the last clause of @target-safe: a closed run's result means what the reference run says. With it, an accepted `Id` lemma makes its two observations equal at runtime.

=== Challenges a proof must meet <sec-referees>

Three independent referees, on 2 November 2026, searched for obstructions to proving Ochr consistent. None found consistency impossible; each found a challenge any proof must meet, and the plan meets each as follows.
- _Truth and proof must meet._ Truth is defined in the set model, which quantifies over every set function, so a proof that reasons only about machine values does not reach it. Ochr can state "$f$ decides halting": with `Prog` a type of two-counter machines, `ExW(Prog → Nat, Decides)` is true in the set model, witnessed by the characteristic function of the halting set, but by no machine value, and an accepted lemma `L3` derives excluded middle for halting from it. A proof over machine values breaks on it: matching on the hypothesis binds the decider to an unknown $sigma_w$, and splitting the stuck $n = seal(sigma_w (P_0))$ needs a head constructor no machine value supplies. In the plan, $sigma_w$ ranges over every function, the halting decider included, and $n$ is generalised to a fresh $sigma_n$ with the path condition $gamma(sigma_n) = gamma(sigma_w)(P_0)$; the split on it covers every assignment, since the value is `Z` or `S` of something. Likewise a lemma `M` stating that every decider answers 0 on a program that halts at once holds at every set-theoretic decider by the outer induction at `M`, not vacuously.
- _A closed proof of `False` on main._ A referee found `EtaBoom` (@sec-risks), and showed that statements relating the generic call to its instances hold only up to an information order. In the plan, `EtaBoom` is a step at which part (a) fails: leaving the arm kept field unknowns whose types were read under that arm's path condition, so in the sibling arm no assignment exists at all, since one field would need an element of $deno(ty("Void")) = emptyset$. Over-approximation was built into @thm-mp through early ends; since D90 the borrows the generic call over-approximates are lent alike at every instance, so the steps are exact.
- _No cycle, and no unchecked code._ A referee found the same gap between truth and proof, the risk of a cycle in which soundness needs a typing invariant that itself needs soundness, and a missing obligation on about 80 unchecked array accesses in the checker. In the plan, truth and proof meet by construction, the typing invariant is part (b) of @thm-mp, proved in the same ordinary induction, and conversion soundness is argued under assignments. The array accesses remain an obligation of the Lean statement, with Q1.

= The principles the checker enforces <sec-principles>

The step lemmas of @thm-mp use a small number of principles, each derived from a model of one part of the calculus (dependent fields, case splitting, observation, recursion, erasure, closing off, locality), and each enforced in the checker at a single place, so that a rule cannot be forgotten by one of several code paths; @sec-route says which step uses which. Every current checker rule corresponds to one of them, and in each area probes looked for a rule the principles demand and the checker lacks. Three such gaps were found on the checker of October 2026, each a closed proof of `False`, and a referee found a fourth, `EtaBoom`, in the worlds principle; all are fixed (@sec-risks). None of the principles is proved.

== Locality <sec-locality>

- (L1) _Names are local._ A running body names only places of its own frame. A closure's captures are copied into its own frame when it is called, and a closure captures no borrow. Global definitions are immutable.
- (L2) _Writes stay in the frame._ A borrow carries its content, so a write through a borrow changes the content inside the borrow held in the running frame; the lender's place does not change until the borrow ends.
- (L3) _Calls exchange only what is passed._ Arguments move into the callee's frame (an owned value is moved out of the caller; a borrow argument carries the caller's loan); the callee returns its result; popping its frame drops its bindings, ending its borrow parameters; [Close] fills only the loans of the call's borrow arguments.
- (L4) _Borrowers sit at or above their loans_, in every run whose effects persist: a loan in frame $f$ is borrowed from $f$ or a frame above it. It follows from no borrows in data, no borrows of borrows, closures capturing no borrow, and [Drop] refusing to let a borrow of a local outlive it. So [Access] and [Drop], which start from a place of the top frame, end only borrowers of the top frame. (L4 fails inside the checker's own private copies, which compute a field's type by binding copies of fields in a scratch frame; those copies are discarded, so nothing leaks.)
- (L5) _Ending is the one channel down._ [End] is how a lender below gets its content back; by (L4) the places a run can change below its frame are exactly the places its frame's borrows were lent from.
- (L6) _Private runs leave no trace._ Statements, and the checker's own scaffolding, run on copies that are thrown away.
- (L7) _Sealed programs are closed, and refinement is not a run._ A sealed program's re-run starts from the empty environment and reads no frame. Refinement substitutes across all frames, but that is the typing judgement acting on the whole state, never the effect of a run.

_Enforced:_ the checker ends a borrower found from a place of the top frame in one function (`endTopLoan`), which asserts (L4) outside private copies; it never fires on the test suite.

== Closing off <sec-closing>

- (C1) _Reconstruction._ The sealed text rebuilds the call exactly: the same function value (code and captures), the other arguments as their values at the call point, each borrowed content in a fresh cell. Nothing else of the call point reaches the callee, by (L1) and (L3). So a sealed program is a closed text whose run depends only on what it embeds and on the refinements of the world it runs in.
- (C2) _Projections._ Each output (the result, and each borrowed place's final content) is its own sealed program, and its final read copies. A `Unit`-typed output is `()`.
- (C3) _Holes are parameters._ A returned borrow's final content is a parameter of every fill, because at the generic call it is not decided which borrowed place the returned borrow points into. A fill's run never inspects its hole: an inert loan is stuck at a match and passes through reads and writes unchanged. Filling it is substitution, which then re-runs the fills.
- (C4) _Re-run exactly when an input changes, everywhere._ A sealed program is re-run when a value it embeds is substituted (a refinement, or the fill of a loan) or a neutral its run derives is refined, at every position a sealed program can occupy: the environment, stored types, the types of abstract values, the goal.
- (C5) _No re-closing._ On a re-run the head call is not closed off again, so a sealed program makes progress or stays exactly as it is.
- (C6) _One text, one value._ Within a world, a sealed program's text determines its value (by (C1) and determinism), so two derivations of one text may be identified.
- (C7) _Errors surface._ A re-run that fails is a type error where it was triggered; exhaustion is undecided.
- (C8) _Clean inputs._ Every input is loan-free and whole when the text is made.
- (C9) _Blocks are calls._ A stuck match or `J` is closed off as a call of an anonymous function of its captures, so (C1)–(C8) apply with the captures as arguments.

_Enforced:_ one function makes sealed programs and one re-runs them; substitution and re-normalisation share one traversal of every value position of the state (`mapState`), which closed a gap in (C4) where re-normalisation had skipped the types of abstract values.

== Worlds <sec-worlds>

Checking a match on an unknown σ of an inductive $ty("D")(overline(a))$ is elimination of a disjoint union: σ denotes an element of $deno(ty("D")(overline(a)))$, which is the union of its constructors' field tuples, and each arm is one summand.

- (X1) _Worlds are exclusive._ No fact established while checking arm $i$ that depends on σ being $ty("C")_i$ is visible while checking arm $j$: a refinement, a type refined by it, a generalisation record, a captured value.
- (X2) _Refinement is total substitution._ Entering a world substitutes σ everywhere it can occur (environment, stored types, types of abstract values, goal, values embedded in sealed programs and closures) and re-normalises. A component skipped keeps a stale, pre-refinement value in a refined world.
- (X3) _Records are scoped._ A generalisation record $n := sigma$ is in force in the world that made it and its sub-worlds (private copies, nested splits), nowhere else: the same text $n$ can denote values of different types in different arms, since it may mention the scrutinee.
- (X4) _The block is checked unrefined._ What follows the match is checked once with σ unknown; its sealed programs resolve when a later split learns σ.

What a world may keep is exactly what holds in every world: fresh-name counters (a counter states no fact) and the types of abstract values created inside the arm (nothing kept names them, since the arm's records are dropped with it). An η-refinement of an abstract value of a one-constructor type is _not_ among them. Its constructor follows from the type alone, but its fields are fresh abstract values whose types are read off σ's type in the arm's world, and can mention what the arm refined: a value parameter, or an earlier field of a dependent constructor. Kept, a sibling arm reused fields typed in another world, a closed proof of `False` (EtaBoom, @sec-risks). So an η-refinement, like a generalisation record, lives until the arm that made it ends, and survives the private copies made within it. _Enforced:_ every arm of every split runs inside one combinator (`inWorld`), whose exit restores every component of the state except those listed, so a new component is restored by default.

== Wholeness <sec-whole>

A dependent constructor $ty("C")(g_1 : A_1, dots, g_k : A_k)$, with $A_j$ mentioning earlier fields, denotes a _Σ-type_: the set of tuples whose later components lie in sets determined by the earlier ones. A borrow of a field, as in Aeneas (the Rust verification tool whose borrow model Ochr's machine follows), receives back when it ends any value of its type.

- (W1) _Open only while the opener runs._ A value may break its telescope only while the code that opened it runs. Wherever it passes to code that relies on its type, it must be whole: read, moved, copied or borrowed whole; captured by a closure, Π-type or block; observed by `Id`; at the end of a borrow of it (a borrow parameter's at its function's return); as a result.
- (W2) _Producer obligation._ The generic path treats every neutral of a dependent type as whole: [Repack] does not look inside it, and [Split] splits it by its telescope. So whatever produces such a neutral must have checked whole the value it stands for. A parameter's σ: the caller passed it whole. A field of a refined neutral: whole because the neutral is. A generalised sealed program: inherited. A stuck call's result or fill: the callee's body checked at its return. A fill with a hole: the same, with the lent part unknown. A stuck block or closed-off `J`: each arm, or the transported term, checked at its end. A hole `?`: an axiom, excluded.
- [Return] is the common form: where a checked body returns, its result and every place it holds by borrow must be whole; a part still lent there is any value of its borrow's type.

_Enforced:_ one function forms every block, taking its arms as computations and running them itself, and calls the same return check that function bodies use; so no block can be formed without the check.

== Erasure and captures <sec-erasure-principles>

- (E1) _Discard._ Every erased term runs on a private copy of the state it would start in, and the copy is thrown away.
- (E2) _Decided by declaration._ Whether a term is erased is read from syntax and declared types. Since sorts are syntactic, "is a proof" is a property of the term itself, so a re-run of a sealed program erases exactly what the direct arm erases.
- (E3) _Only copy-independent facts survive a copy:_ name counters, the types of names created in it, generalisation records (keyed by closed program texts, which mean the same in any state), η-refinements (the fields' types are the copy's world's, which the copy shares), checked recursion obligations, caches.
- (E4) _In place means within the statement's copy._ A borrow-typed argument of a statement runs in place, not on its own copy, but the statement call is itself erased.
- (E5) _Reads move inside statements as at runtime,_ on the copy only: a side that reads $x$ and then observes $x$ sees `⊥`, which is compared as a value.
- (K1) _Captures over-approximate._ For each outer place and each arm, a block's capture mode is at least the arm's effect on it, in the order none ⊑ copy ⊑ write or borrow ⊑ move, measured at [Join]'s grain and net of the arm's own refills; a closure or Π-type in an arm moves what it mentions. The places a block takes by move are [Join]'s set, so they are the same on every path.
- (K2) _Over-approximation is shared._ A block's moves are exact, by [Join]. Its captures by borrow can over-approximate an arm's, which only removes options for the code after the block; since D90 a direct run makes the same accesses at the match's entry ([Entry]), so the borrows the block's captures end are ended on every path.
- (K3) _Effects are measured where they happen:_ a move inside a nested block is that block's, and a move inside a statement is discarded with its copy. By (E2) a block's re-run decides as the direct arm does.

_Enforced:_ statement positions run through one combinator (`inStatement`) and the checker's scaffolding through another (`privately`); captures are computed by one function (`blockCaptures`).

== Recursion <sec-rec>

A definition $kw("fix") f (overline(x) : overline(A)) : B kw("by") x_j := b$ denotes the function defined by well-founded recursion on the size of the entry value of $x_j$.
- (R1) _The measure is the entry value, as refined._ Each recursive call's argument (its content, through a borrow) is compared with the caller's entry value as refined so far, whatever the body did to the place since. Strict subterm implies smaller size. Refinement only adds constructors, so a strict subterm at the generic call is one at every instance.
- (R2) _Recursive calls are named._ $f$ is reached only through the name it binds for itself, or a nested function's capture of that name; which calls recurse is a fact about syntax, never decided by comparing function values, which refinement changes.
- (R3) _Check before running._
- (R4) _One order._ A definition sees only earlier definitions and itself, so every chain of calls is ordered lexicographically by (definition, entry size). A call through a function value is not a recursive call unless its head is the name; a closure formed in $f$'s body that calls $f$ is checked against $f$'s entry value where it is formed.
- (R5) _Types recurse like terms_, and a statement never mentions the definition being checked.
- (R6) _Runs only unfold._ Untyped runs unfold definitions without the check; they decide nothing about well-foundedness, and a run that does not terminate is exhaustion, never a decision.

_Enforced:_ one function decides whether a call belongs to a recursion entry, by a mark on the binding (`recFrameOf`).

== Observation <sec-obs-principles>

- (O1) _Independence._ The two sides of `Id` run from the same state on independent copies, in the same world.
- (O2) _Resolved, and where re-pointed borrows point._ Observations compare resolved values, so where a borrow sits does not matter, with one exception: a borrow binding that both sides re-point is observed by where it points, by writing a fresh value through it ([Obs-ref]), since a continuation can write through it.
- (O3) _Footprint and type-state._ A position whose resolved content cannot differ between the sides need not be observed. It can differ only if a side writes it, directly or through a borrow, so the owners of every place written or borrowed, and of every borrow variable handed on whole, are observed. A place only read is not observed: whether a side moves it, consumes, re-points or leaves open a borrow binding, or ends a borrow held outside it, is the type-state's ([Id-typestate]), on which both sides must agree for `Id` to be well formed.
- (O4) _All owners._ A loan can sit in several owned positions (a hole in several fills); each is observed.
- (O5) _One order._ The conjunction is ordered by syntax, the same on both paths, since `∧` is not commutative by conversion.

*Footprints at a call site.* A footprint's roots are read off syntax, so they are the same on both paths; only the owners can differ. At the generic call a borrow parameter's loan sits in one fresh cell; at an instance it may sit in several owners (O4). Every extra owner's resolved content is a function of what was written through the borrow plus parts no side writes, so if the generic statement proves the written values equal, each extra owner's equation is true too. This is footprint coverage (@sec-wf), with the injectivity of contexts.

= Tests before mechanisation <sec-tests>

The route is checked on paper before anything is mechanised, hardest first, following the standing preference to attack first the part most likely to fail. Four tests were planned: tests 0 and 1 independent of each other, the census audit cheap, and test 2 after test 0. Each result is summarised here; the full write-ups are the documents named. All four have passed. The one failure they found is a fifth closed proof of `False`, in which the generic call and an instance disagreed on whether a place is moved (@sec-risks); [Join] (D84) rules it out. A fifth test, test 3, checked type safety (@target-safe) on the slice, and found no checker bug.

== Test 0: the model exists, on a slice <sec-test-model>

*What it checks.* That the set model, stratified by universe, can be defined without circularity on a slice containing each feature that makes the definition hard: `Nat`; a type function defined by recursion on data (`Cells(E, n)`); a type variable $A : ty("Type")_0$ with $R$ at it; a function type returning a borrow, with injective backward functions; and a Π into `Prop` over `Type₀`.

*What would fail it.* A clause with nothing smaller to recurse on, or a meaning that is not a set. Then the plan fails at its base.

*Result: the model exists on the slice* (`ochr/docs/24`, 6 November 2026; a paper result, most of it also encoded in Lean in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/Model/Slice.lean`, which builds with no `sorry` and uses only the axioms `propext` and `Quot.sound`). Neither apparent circularity of the definition is an obstacle.
- _A type means only its good elements._ If a function type returning a borrow means only the functions whose backward functions are injective, then $R$ at every type is the meaning itself, and the meaning and $R$ are one definition. A type variable means whatever set the assignment gives it, and $R$ at it is that whole set: an Ochr program can use a value of a type variable as a function only after transporting along a proof that the variable _is_ some Π-type, whose meaning is already a good set. Pairing each type with a separate set of good elements would be not just unneeded but harmful: the Lean encoding found two functions that agree on every good argument yet differ at a junk one, so with pairs two convertible functions would have different meanings and [Conv-fun] would be unsound. With carriers holding only good elements, [Conv-fun] is function extensionality, proved in Lean.
- _Meanings are defined on terms, not on type values._ The recursion is on the position of the item in the program, then the structure of the term, then, inside a recursive function, the rank of its decreasing argument; all three are well founded. A Π-type's codomain _term_ is a subterm of the Π-term even though the Π-type value computes the codomain by running code; a computed type $F(a)$ means the meaning of an earlier item at the meaning of $a$; a type variable is looked up, not recursed into. $ty("Type")_i$ means $V_(kappa_i)$ outright, so there is no universe of codes and no induction–recursion; universe levels matter only for sizes (that a type at level $i$ means a member of $V_(kappa_i)$), which is part of membership, not of the definition. The Lean encoding needs its levels bounded (Q1).
- _A recursive function means a function whether or not it passed [Rec]._ Its meaning is defined by _guarded_ recursion: a recursive call whose decreasing argument does not drop in rank means the error value. So [Rec] is not needed for the definition; it is what makes the guard never fire, which is part of membership, (b). The defining equation under that hypothesis is proved in Lean for type functions recursive on `Nat`. So [Rec] belongs to membership (@thm-mp, @sec-wf).

*What it leaves.* The meaning of the state (live borrows as functions of their final contents, holes, inert loans, `⊥`) and therefore of `Id` inside types, the next hardest piece, which test 2 took up. Two obligations come with it: a type's meaning never depends on a hole, and every accepted closure returning a borrow has an injective backward function (part of membership). In a typed mechanisation, meaning and membership must be built together, as the Lean encoding already does for borrow-returning functions.

== Test 1: the known proofs of `False` are failed steps <sec-test-bugs>

*What it checks.* For each closed proof of `False` the checker once accepted (@sec-risks), and for the moved-out equation the test `MovedBothSides` guards, whether a part of @thm-mp fails at the buggy step and holds once the fix is in. A bug that no part catches would mean the theorem is too weak.

*Result: all five are explained, and none escapes* (`ochr/docs/25`, 6 November 2026; a paper analysis, the buggy behaviour taken from each fix's commit).
- _A returned borrow of an index field_ (`BoomI2`). [Repack] at the function's return checked the owner whole with the lent field read at its current content. Its accepting direction owes (b) for the owner under _every_ future of the returned borrow's hole, and the caller writes another value there, at which the record's dependent field no longer fits. (b) fails; the fix, checking the lent part as an unknown of its type, restores it.
- _A stuck `J` closed off unchecked_ (`BoomJ`). The fill left in the borrowed place means exactly what running the `J` means, so (a) holds even before the fix. (b) fails for the fill: where the endpoints are equal, it means a record whose dependent field was typed at the old index. The fix, checking each arm of a block at its end, is (b) at a producer of a sealed value.
- _A recursive closure capturing a value it splits on_ (`BoomCapSplit`). The split keeps meaning: the refined closure means the same function, so (a) holds. [Rec] identified recursive calls by comparing function values, which the split changed without changing their meaning, so a recursive call went unchecked, and (b) fails: under guarded recursion (test 0) that call's decreasing argument does not drop, so the guard fires and the closure's meaning there is the error value, which lies in no type. The fix, recognising recursive calls by name, is syntax, which refinement does not change.
- _`EtaBoom`._ Leaving the arm $n := ty("Z")$ kept an η-refinement whose fields were typed in that arm. In the sibling arm the field of type `Void` forced every assignment to choose an element of $emptyset$, so the arm admitted no assignment at all and could prove anything. (a) fails in its coverage part, the case the route predicted. The fix drops the refinement with its arm.
- _`MovedBothSides`._ At the generic call a stuck block on each side of an `Id` takes the same place by move. Without [Join], an instance runs one arm and may leave the place whole on one side, so equating the two `⊥` fails (a). With [Join], every path moves the place on both sides, the two `⊥` mean the same state, and the test is accepted and true.
Five further soundness guards found by ablating the checker fit the same way: a match on `Z`/`S` needs a `Nat` scrutinee (coverage, in (a)); a whole read repacks, a proof field assigned while part of its value is lent is checked under every future, and each arm of a block is checked at its end (all (b)); a borrow is formed only at `Type₀` ((b) for types, and (c)).

*What it shows.* Part (a) alone catches one of the five. Three are caught by (b): two quantified over the futures of the live holes, one by the guard of a recursive function. In all three the step's (a) holds even before the fix. So the route stands or falls with (b) as much as with (a), and must be presented, as @thm-mp is, as one induction over all three, never as "each step keeps meaning" alone. Each bug is local to one step and one part of the theorem. A fifth closed proof of `False`, found after this test, is of `MovedBothSides`'s kind, a failure of (a) where the generic call and an instance disagree on whether a place is moved, and [Join] removes it (@sec-risks).

== The census of the checker's decisions <sec-test-census>

*What it checks.* Every decision the checker makes, as listed by the two-paths census (`ochr/core/notes/two-paths-census.md`), with every edge case found by ablating the checker and every remaining site that reads a value's own type (`valType?`), sorted into the kinds of @sec-wf: same, aligned, less informative, or check.

*Result: everything sorts, apart from one item* (`ochr/docs/26`, 6 November 2026; read-only, on main with D80 and D81). The audit raised two; a probe has since settled the first.
- _A stuck block's moves._ A stuck block takes a place by move when some arm moves it, even if another arm writes it. [Join] makes the direct run drop the same places, read from syntax and declared types, so a block's moves are aligned, not an over-approximation, and `⊥` means _moved_ (@def-state-meaning).
- _Same:_ the η family, a proof's `⋆`, `Eq` and `J` computing, splits and records, the fill of a lent `Unit`. _Aligned:_ erasure, a function type's class, a statement argument's place, [Close]'s row, copy or move, all read from syntax since D77 and D80; a match's moves at its join, and their grain ([Join]); the type-state of an `Id` and [Obs-ref]'s set (census site 28, D84). _Less informative:_ early ends, where a block's captures by borrow or a stuck call's fills over-approximate a run's loans. _Checks:_ the rest, each establishing (b), establishing (c), or protecting only runtime safety (the requirement that borrows passed to a call that closes off be whole). [Id-typestate]'s no-ending clause is a check judged on the checked run (site 28, class (c)): it only rejects. "A returned borrow must be whole" is membership, not only runtime safety (test 2).
- _The "same" kind rests on five lemmas, not one per site:_ membership; the soundness of `valType?`; footprint coverage; conversion soundness (@lem-conv); and well-formedness (@sec-wf).
- _Item 1, settled by a probe: a simplification of the proof, not a gap._ When a value is checked whole while part of it is lent, the rule checks the lent part as an unknown of the borrow's _declared_ type, since the borrow may still write anything of that type there. The checker instead uses the type read off the lent part's current content (`lentUnknown`). The audit worried about the case where that type cannot be read. A probe found that this case rejects rather than accepts: the loan stays in the value, and [Repack]'s check reads the same unreadable type and reports that the part is not of its type. The case is also effectively unreachable, since a lent index field is first-order data, whose content's type can always be read (a constructor value records its parameters, an unknown has a stored type, a sealed program has its head's codomain); four attacks and an attempted closed proof of `False` were all rejected by [Repack]. Reading the borrow's declared type, as the rule says, would change no verdict. It would only remove this check's dependence on the soundness of `valType?`, one of the five lemmas behind "same".
- _Item 2, open: the class of an embedded call head._ When a call's head is a value with no written type, such as a sealed function value, its class is read off the value's Π-type. That agrees with the declared position only because Π-types of different class are not convertible (D54), and no test exercises that check. The fix: a witness test, or carrying the class with the value, as D80 does for captures.
- _Untested invariant checks:_ that a closure in a block arm captures nothing the block borrows, in the copy-type case, and that a recursive function occurs in its body only as a call's head. Each needs a test that it rejects something.

== Test 2: a higher-order slice <sec-test-ho>

*What it checks.* The route on a slice with the features that the first-order fragment F leaves out (F is the part of the calculus for which the Ochr paper proves soundness: no closures, no function parameters, no type families): function parameters ranging over every set function, a closure built in a run and called later, [Conv-fun] (functions compared by their generic observations), and a function taking a function that returns a borrow. It checks three cases that are delicate for any proof about this machine (an early end chosen by a branch, as in `Pick`; early ends with pairs after a [Drop]; and resolution commuting with refinement through inert loans), and the census's reading of `⊥` on higher-order code.

*Why not F.* F is first-order: no closures, no function parameters, no type families. Redoing its proof this way is at most a warm-up, and its outcome says little about the whole calculus.

*Result: states have a meaning, and the step lemmas hold on the slice* (`ochr/docs/27`, 6 November 2026; a paper result, with the meaning of states checked in Lean on a toy symbolic machine in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/Model/State.lean` and `State2.lean`, which build with no `sorry` and use only the axioms `propext` and `Quot.sound`). With it, all four planned tests have passed; the one failure this test found is a fifth closed proof of `False`, which [Join] (D84) rules out.
- _A state means a function of a future._ A future gives each live borrow its final content. A place that lent its content means its final content; a place holding the borrow means its current content, which is what a read through it sees; a sealed fill means its program, with its hole read from the future; `⊥` means _moved_. Ending a borrow fixes the future at the holder's current content and forgets the holder, so it loses only information, and that is what the checker's [End] does by substitution. The Lean check found this shape necessary: a single function from final contents to resolved contents cannot express a read through a borrow.
- _`Id`'s meaning._ Each observation ends every live borrow of its copy, so it depends on the assignment alone, and `Id` compares the results and every position neither side moves. The checker's conjunction over the footprint's owners is then exact, given footprint coverage.
- _Both obligations test 0 left hold, with no new rule._ No stored type holds a live loan: types are formed on private copies after [Access] has ended the loans in what they read, and the one type computed over a lent field, an open dependent value's, quantifies over the hole, as D64 amended and D73 require. Every accepted function returning a borrow has an injective backward function, by an invariant that every hole is placed injectively in the state. [Access], D67 (an assignment ends the loans it overwrites), D65 (a borrow of a dying local cannot escape) and (C8) (arguments hole-free at [Close]) keep it, and every called function is good because types mean only good elements.
- _The three delicate cases are routine._ `Pick`'s early end is a definition (a fill is a function of its hole) and an end that loses only information. Early ends with pairs after a [Drop] are an end, which is below whatever the shape of the ended borrow's content, since reborrows travel with it and are read from the same future. And resolution commuting with refinement through inert loans is meaning preservation for a fill's re-run, with the hole as one more unknown.
- _Monotonicity._ `Eq`, `J`, matches and [Close]'s rows are monotone in the order between state meanings that the theorem then used (for every future of the lower state, a future of the upper at which every position is below); @sec-wf says what the simulation form asks in its place. The test found a fifth closed proof of `False` (@sec-risks), in which conversion identified two `⊥` that the generic call and an instance did not agree on; [Join] makes them agree, so `⊥` is compared as a value.
- _The form of the main theorem._ The checker's run is below a _reference run_ of the term on meanings (@def-refrun): the denotation as a state transformer, which applies the meanings of functions at calls instead of running their bodies, so it is not a second machine run. Each step is exact, or an end the reference does not make. @thm-mp keeps the reference run and the two kinds of step, and compares the two runs by a simulation (@def-sim) in place of the order this test used, which the mechanisation found false once a borrow has been held (@sec-spine).
- _The higher-order slice._ A function parameter means any good function, non-computable ones included, and a call through it closes off exactly. A closure built in a run is fixed when it is formed, since its captures hold no loan, and a later call applies its meaning. [Conv-fun] is sound by function extensionality on good functions. A function taking a function that returns a borrow, `UseG (g : Π(x : &Nat). &Nat) (a : Nat) : Nat := (let r = g(&a); *r := 5; clone(a))`, means $(G, n) |-> "back"_G (n)(5)$, so two different writes give two different results, by injectivity.
- _In Lean._ [Access] is derived the checker's way; ending one borrow reaches every reborrow, and shallow ends are sound, so early ends with pairs need nothing more; nested holes hold; and [Drop] loses only information. (WF5), loan-free arguments at a call, turns out to be about causality: with a live loan in an argument, a read now would depend on a write made later, so the meaning of a state needs it, not only runtime safety. Lean's own gaps: the step lemmas at field paths are checked only on the runs it encodes, and the preservation of (WF2) is assumed.
- _A correction to the census._ "A returned borrow must be whole" is membership, not only runtime safety: a returned borrow holding `⊥` has a forward value with no meaning. The requirement that borrows passed to a call that closes off be whole stays on the runtime side.

*What it leaves.* The lemmas this test assumed or argued without checking: footprint coverage; the soundness of `valType?`; the preservation of well-formedness, where (WF2) needs (WF3); [Open] and [Repack] beyond the lent-index case; and erased runs inside the reference run. These are the residuals of @sec-order.

== Test 3: type safety on the higher-order slice <sec-test-safety>

*What it checks.* @target-safe on test 2's higher-order slice: error reflection, runtime `J`, erasure and termination, for closed runs of accepted data functions.

*What would fail it.* A closed run of an accepted function that goes wrong where the reference run does not, or whose result differs from the reference run's.

*Result: type safety holds on the slice, up to two open lemmas, and no checker bug was found* (`ochr/docs/29-type-safety-slice.typ`; checked in part in Lean in `Ochr/Model/Safety.lean`, a concrete runtime on test 2's toy machine, which builds with no `sorry` and uses only the axioms `propext` and `Quot.sound`). It is stated against [Join] (D84), under which a moved place means _moved_ on every path, and moves are exact between the generic call, a closed run and the reference run.
- _The target, sharpened._ Function arguments must be built by accepted code, since a closure from outside the program could loop; proof parameters must be true in the model, which also makes runtime `J` reachable at non-convertible endpoints. @target-safe is stated so.
- _Error reflection._ Of the machine's 140 error sites, value errors (11) reflect to the reference run, since at a closed state a value means itself; borrow errors (18) reflect to it too, operation by operation in Lean, using the injectivity of backward functions, except at one-element types, where a loan can sit that no meaning depends on (`UseDirect`); there they reflect to the checker's derivation, by a lemma about borrow structure that is sketched. Static and typing errors (93) are unreachable at runtime, and internal assertions (18) are invariants. Following `*` from a non-borrow needs typing in the invariant.
- _Runtime `J`._ The checker's rule gets stuck at runtime on two encodings of one function ($n + 0$ against $0 + n$ in Lean); returning the transported term is sound by membership and reads nothing (Lean: `castRT_safe`). This is [J-run] (D85).
- _Erasure_ holds by construction, with runtime `J` its one extra clause. _Termination_ holds for recursion by name; calls through function values need a predicative realizability relation, not yet built. _Well-formedness_ at runtime is partly proved in Lean.
- _Probes._ The likeliest failure of the borrow-structure lemma, η at `Unit` in a closed-off call's fills, does not occur: two programs aimed at it are rejected, as predicted.

*What it leaves.* The open lemmas listed in @sec-safety.

== Audit: what a context can observe <sec-test-adeq>

*What it checks.* For `Id` adequacy, a target since dropped: every channel through which a context can observe a term (its result, the places it writes or moves, the borrows it consumes or ends, where a borrow it assigns points, and any other), compared with what `Id` relates.

*What would fail it.* A channel `Id` neither observes nor rules out by the type-state.

*Result: no closed proof of `False`, and `Id` adequacy's typed form* (`ochr/docs/30-id-adequacy-audit.md`, 9 November 2026). In every gap `Id`'s meaning is computed soundly, and what fails is substitution. Over every accepted position, with errors observable, the in-place idiom fails (`AddMZero`: one side consumes a borrow binding the other leaves), and closing that channel inside `Id` would reject 81 of the suite's verdicts, the paper's first lemma among them. So `Id` adequacy was stated over contexts where both fillings are accepted. There the one value channel left is where a re-pointed borrow binding points, which [Obs-ref] observes at no cost to the suite; [Id-typestate], with its no-ending clause, makes every error channel the same for both fillings, the resource lemma that was dropped with the target. The checker's D84 implements both.

= The mechanisation so far <sec-spine>

A Lean development of @thm-mp against the checker's own types (`Value`, `Term`, `MState`) is under way. It is on the branch `spine`, in `ochr/core/lean/Ochr/Meaning/` (the library `OchrMeaning`); none of it is on main yet. Its design note, `ochr/docs/31-meaning-spine.md` on that branch, records every decision, statement and counterexample, and its section "Where things stand" is the current status. This section summarises it as of commit `96f1c2fe` (21 November 2026), on main's D94, on main's D93, after the rebase onto D90 and D91 and the change to self-describing data values (D32). File names below are relative to `Ochr/Meaning/`.

*What it aims at.* The statement `consistencyBounded` (`Bounded.lean`): the Lean statement of @target with the added hypothesis that the program writes no universe above `Type₁` (Q1). It is assembled in `Consistency.lean` from the step lemmas in seven statements, all unproved: an accepted program resolves to items checked in order; every accepted definition means a member of its type (@cor-mean, `defn_member`); the library's `False` means the empty proposition; a data model exists for every accepted program; and consistency follows.

*What is defined.* Meanings of values and states, with elements typed by universe level and constructor values describing themselves (`Sem.lean`, `Value.lean`, `State.lean`); the data model, as an interface with a generic construction by stages (`Data.lean`, `DataGeneric.lean`); well-formedness and membership (`WF.lean`); the reference run, for every term form the checker runs (`Run.lean`), with D90's tail position, [Entry] at non-tail matches and at `J` (the stuck block's captures as a decision, read statically: `BlockCaps.lean`), a borrow-typed match's lending, and a closure's capture accesses, and with the meaning of the code in a state read through it; the meaning of a program (`Program.lean`); and the simulation relation (`Rel`, in `Steps/Statements.lean`).

*What is proved,* with no `sorry`, on Lean's three standard axioms:
- _The four core lemmas_ (`Lemmas.lean`), on which every step that substitutes into a value or re-reads one rests. `meanV_substLoan` and `run_substLoan`: filling a loan with a value is fixing the future at that label to the value's meaning, for a value's meaning and for a term's reference run. This is what makes the checker's [End], a substitution, the end of @def-lessinfo. `meanV_congr` and `run_congr`: a value's meaning reads the future only at the loans the value holds, and a term's run only at the labels its term and starting state can reach. They were re-proved four times as the reference run changed.
- _About the relation_ (`Steps/Sim.lean`), the relation of @def-sim with lending and no early ends: it holds at the start, with no hypothesis (`Rel.start`); steps stated in it compose (`sim_seq`); and it survives a private copy with the same reference state and renaming (`Rel.private_copy`), because no label at or above the counter of the state the copy started from occurs after it (`private_copy_no_label`, from (WF11)).
- _About the drop at the join_ (`Steps/JoinDropLib.lean`): the checker's loop over the join's list taken apart into its steps, and the reference's drop keeping the scope the list is read in. The step for the whole drop (`joinDrop_step`) is reduced to the drop of one place (`dropOne_step`, open) by induction on the list; it is not proved, since the drop of one place is not.
- _About the model_: a type value with no loan reads as the same type, with the same shape and parameters, at every future (`ShapeAgree.tyStr_futureFree`), so the checker's reading of a stored type at a fixed future is the reading at the run's; a constructor value's tag and fields are read off its meaning, whatever its fields hold and whatever type it is held at (`DataModel.parts_ctorM`, `DataOfChoice.tag_right`); a partly moved value is a member of its type (`PartlyMoved.pmem_partly`); the places already moved at a match are read the same way whatever the future at the labels they do not read (`movedPlaces_congr`); and (WF11) gives the statement that stored types hold no loan (`types_holeFree`).
- _About the decisions_ (`Steps/Decisions.lean`): each of the eight statements that a decision of the reference run is the checker's follows from the run context's hypothesis (its decisions are the checker's at the state) and the simulation of the checker's decision procedures between states that agree on what they read (`DecideScope.lean`, whose relation now also ignores the move log and the refinements), the join's moves and `Id`'s re-pointed set included.
- _About [Entry]_ (`Regress/EntryCheck.lean`): on the checker's own state at a match not in tail position, on a known constructor, whose arm that does not run assigns a lent place, the reference's captures are the checker's, and its entry ends the same borrow, leaving the same holders and the same sites; the definition that writes through the borrow afterwards is rejected, and with the match in tail position accepted. And at a read of one field while another is lent (`Regress/OneCtorEntry.lean`: `let r = &x.1; let y = clone(x.0); *r := Z`, on a known pair and on a parameter, both accepted), the checker's read and the reference's both keep the borrow live with the same holder and site.
- _About the assignment_ (`AssignCongr.lean`): a term's reference run and a value's meaning read the assignment and Δ only at the unknowns they hold (`run_congrA`, `meanV_congrA`), so a step that creates unknowns can extend both without changing any old meaning.
- _About ends_ (`Mono.lean`, `RelendComm.lean`): an end, and an access, which is a sequence of ends, lose only information (`SState.endLabel_below`, `SState.access_below`); two ends commute, in meaning, when the borrows do not depend on each other, which (WF3) gives, and in where the borrows are lent when neither is lent at the other's holder (`relend_comm`).
- _About the drop of one place_ (`BotWrite.lean`, `SitesWrite.lean`, `Steps/DropOne.lean`, `Steps/MovedPlace.lean`): writing `⊥` at a path commutes with the meaning (`mean_updAt_bot`) and removes exactly the sites at or under the path (`sitesOf_updAt_bot`); the checker's position of a place is the reference's (`rel_resolve`). The drop of one place is assembled from named obligations, which are open: the checker's re-rooting of a place that does not exist (`movedPlace`, which η-refines, generalises or accesses a parent whole) is an induction-hypothesis field of its own, assembled branch by branch along the checker's code, and the refinement and the generalisation it uses are assembled the same way. The in-place refinement of an unknown of a one-constructor type (`etaInPlace`), which since D93 also refines the scrutinee of a one-constructor match and a stuck block's captures, is assembled from its recorded and fresh forms and the substitution (`etaInPlace_sim`). Since main's D94 the re-rooting ends by returning the place if it exists, a prefix holding `⊥` or `⋆` for a no-op, or rejecting the definition, so it needs no argument about the parent's content. Where the place exists, the drop is proved in its four cases: where the checker's place is `⋆` nothing happens on either side, and where it is `⊥` the reference accesses it, which ends nothing, and writes `moved` over `moved` ((WF14), and `content_follow`: along every place at the join's grain the reference reads the meaning of the checker's value there, `followMean`); a borrow at a root ends on both sides; a copy is not dropped; and data is accessed and written `⊥` on both, with the relation and `Good` after the write (`botWrite_rel`, `botWrite_good`, `botWrite_hyps`; the write beside a closed-off call, `mean_updAt_bot_sealed`). Where the re-rooting returned a prefix holding `⊥` or `⋆`, nothing happens on either side, proved the same way (`joinDropOne_noop`). The reference's drop decides as the checker does: it tests a holder first, never reads `moved` from a meaning, and reads the place after its own access, as every read of a place does. What the drop of one place still rests on: three facts of typing (the reference reads no data of a non-copy place as a proof; the checker's access of a root holding data puts no proof there; the join derefs no proof below a variable), that an access loses no unknown, the copy decision's alignment (which waits on main's measurement of the caches), and the run context after each step. The decisions are shown to read only Σ, Δ, the reading mode, the top frame's readings and two caches, so they survive every change of the environment's values, the move log and the records; a step that creates unknowns needs more, since they read the types of the unknowns in the values they inspect. The lead's answer, that the context's decisions are extended with its Δ, is stated (`Steps/DecExtend.lean`) and not built on, after two findings: the unknowns grow at ends of borrows too, and a refinement rewrites the types of existing unknowns, so a decision about old unknowns can change.
- The design note also records as proved: that a term whose tails read no expected type runs the same whatever type its context expects (`run_hintIndep`, `HintIndep.lean`); the step for [Close] on test 2's central case, a stuck call through a function parameter that returns a borrow, whose result and fill mean the forward value and the backward function of the callee's meaning (`Attack/CloseCallFwd.lean`, `Attack/CloseCallBack.lean`); and libraries for threading the checker's state monad through a proof (`PosLib.lean`, `RunLib.lean`).

*What is stated and unproved.* Every step lemma: 77 statements in `Steps/Statements.lean`, each indexed by the checker's step budget, bundled in one record (`StepsIH`, `Steps/IH.lean`), with one theorem per statement in the area files `Steps/Borrow.lean`, `Subst.lean`, `Eq.lean`, `Call.lean`, `Match.lean`, `Obs.lean`, `Types.lean`, `WF.lean`, `Eval.lean`, `Tail.lean` and `Decisions.lean`, and the induction that ties them in `Steps/Knot.lean`. They include the residual lemmas test 2 left: footprint coverage (`footprint_coverage_stmt`), the soundness of `valType?` (`valType_sound_stmt`), the preservation of well-formedness, [Repack]'s accepting direction (`repackCheck_sound_stmt`), the recursion check and conversion. No step lemma is proved. Seventeen are in the simulation form: evaluation and `let` (`eval_step_stmt`, `evalCore_step_stmt`, `letIn_step_stmt`); [Access] and [End] (`access_step_stmt`, `endBorrow_step_stmt`); the matches (`evalMatch_step_stmt`, `evalMatchInd_step_stmt`, `evalMatchByType_step_stmt`, `closeBlock_step_stmt`) and the drop at the join (`joinEntry_stmt`, `dropOne_step_stmt`, `joinDrop_step_stmt`, the last reduced to the one before it); `Id`, `Eq` and observation (`observe_step_stmt`, `footprint_coverage_stmt`, `idType_step_stmt`, `eq_step_stmt`); and a function type's shape (`piShape_step_stmt`). The rest still have the order form, which is false wherever a borrow has been held, and no proof should be started against one before it is restated. The drop at the join and `Id` have been _attacked_ from a simulating state and restated (@sec-wf); neither is proved. One lemma about the reference run is also stated and unproved, believed true: that its result does not depend on the starting label counter (`run_counter_indep`). The other stated beside it, that it reads the assignment only at the unknowns its term mentions (`run_abs_congr`), is proved (above).

*What stating it found.* An attempt to prove the step lemma judged most likely to be false, the re-run of a sealed program, could not derive it from the induction hypothesis, and each reason was a fault of the model's definitions or of a statement. None was a checker bug. Each is recorded in the design note, most with a Lean file that checks it.
- _The meaning of code was read at one fixed future_, so a state's sealed programs were read at the wrong one, and the step for [End] had no witness on a lender holding a fill. Fixed: code is read at the assignment and future asked (the induction, after @thm-mp).
- _The reference run failed on accepted programs_, six kinds of them, and differed from the checker at sixteen further points; eleven of those are fixed and five are open (@def-refrun).
- _Statements were false at parameters they left free_, which the checker trusts typing or its own state for: a context's globals, the type of a callee, the kind and result of a closed-off call. Fixed by the hypothesis that the context is the state's (the run context, after @thm-mp) and by reading the callee through the run.
- _An assignment was owed an element at every recorded unknown_, including those of discarded arms. Fixed: only at the unknowns the state references (@def-assign).
- _A value in flight had no type_, so a future could give its label anything and its lender meant the error value; and a returned borrow, being in flight, is not in the state, so the state after a borrow failed (WF2). Fixed: the invariant counts a value in flight as part of the state and types its label.
- _The reference run leaked labels._ A call consumed the holder of every labelled argument but fixed the final content only of the borrow parameters', and a `let`'s drop ended the label its binding's type carried, not the one its position held. Both refuted the core lemmas as first stated, and are fixed.
- _The order form of part (a) was false and did not compose._ Replaced by the simulation (@def-sim).
- _The relation itself was wrong four times since_ (@def-sim): it left out a binding's function shape, so it admitted states from which every call through a variable fails; it asked a renaming of every label issued, which does not exist after a statement that borrows; it compared by order, which admitted states from which a match's join and `Id` differ; and it did not pin the reference state at futures where an early-ended label has another value, which [Access] read (repaired first by a loan witness; since D90 the reference reads lending statically, as the checker does, and the relation compares where each borrow is lent instead).
- _Running the first step proof found a checker fault_, accepted functions whose closed calls fail (`EJ`, then `EI` and `G1`): the generic call ended borrows an instance did not. The checker now decides when a borrow ends the same way on every path (D90, D91). Proving the drop at the join further found that a step's run nested in a `do` block was not covered by the statements (a run from any log fixes it), that a step creating unknowns must hand on a context with their types (the induction hypothesis is now at every context), and that the drop's statement needs the join's grain of its list.
- _A partly moved value was a member of no type_, so membership failed after every move of a field; and _a constructor value with a field outside its type meant the error value_, so reading one field of a pair ended a borrow of the other. Both fixed (@def-state-meaning, @thm-mp).
- _The reference run differed from the checker at five more points_, found by attacking the first steps (@def-refrun): three fixed (the grain of the places already moved at a match, [Obs-ref]'s written value, a `let`-bound type alias) and two open (a function type computed by a call, and a proof's type), beside the two known before (a call's result and a field).
- _The well-formedness invariant gained clauses_: (WF11), without which nothing excluded a label issued inside a private copy from surviving it, and (WF12), what lending needs (@sec-wf). The sweep of the checker's states then found three clauses wrong as first stated, none a checker fault, and they were restated to what they are for (@sec-wf); after D93 it found the restated (WF12) too strong (two borrows filled at one place, `Regress/TwoFills.lean`), and it is restated to what the absorption of a lent place needs.
- _The model read a data value with information from outside it_: first a declaration guessed from the set (two declarations with one set took the wrong arm of a match), then the type its earlier fields give (a drop after a moved index, and a read after a rewritten one, did not find the field's own value; both programs accepted). Fixed by making a data value describe itself (docs/31 D31, D32); the four core lemmas are re-proved on it.
- _The path condition left out the split's refinements_, which the checker reads back into the state (a program generalised before in the world becomes its σ with the refinements since substituted): a state meeting every other condition had a program replaced by a value meaning something else (`Regress/RefsPathCond.lean`). Fixed: each refinement in force is a path condition (@def-assign).
- _The generalisation step asked nothing of the value generalised_: generalising a proof at a number place recorded a proof as a number (`Regress/GenNeutralContent.lean`). Fixed with the hypotheses both callers have: the value is the place's content, a sealed program holding no loan. And the naming half of (WF6) let the counter of unknowns run ahead of their types, so a generalisation could leave a value holding an untyped unknown; the counter is now the number of typed unknowns, which the checker keeps.
- _The reference run lacked D90's entry and tail position_: built (above). Building it found one form of the entry the model did not have: the checker ran a read of a field `q.i` as a one-arm match on `q` (D81) whose entry read `q` whole, so reading `x.0` ended a borrow of `x.1` (D90's known gap F4). The checker was changed, not the model (D93): a match on a one-constructor type has no entry and no join, and the reference decides that by the match's declaration, as the checker does.
- _The naming half of (WF6) left records and stored types unbounded_: a record naming the counter met every clause, and a fresh unknown then took that name and was pinned by the record (`Regress/FreshBound.lean`). Fixed: every unknown the state names has a type. _And ends create unknowns_: an end of a borrow of a dependent type checks its content against the type again ([Repack]) and keeps an unknown that nothing names (394 of 24,870 ends run from the suite's recorded states; none adds a record or names the unknown afterwards). So the run context is asked for Δ only at the unknowns the state names, as the assignment already was.
- _The re-rooting of a place to drop can stop at a parent where the reference drops less_ (docs/31 D41). When a strict prefix of the parent is lent, the re-rooting accesses it, which can give the parent a constructor value through which the place now exists; the checker drops the parent whole, the reference the place (`Regress/ReRootExists.lean`; the join does not reach this on any program found: an arm that lends the prefix uses it whole, so the join moves it whole). And an arm the checker does not run is never checked, so a place it moves can be ill-typed (`(c.0).5` where the constructor has two fields): the checker drops `c.0` whole and the reference nothing, on an accepted program (`Regress/UnrunArmPlace.lean`). The checker is the conservative side in both, so nothing unsound is accepted, but the drop's statement is false there. Proposed to main: the re-rooting returns the place itself wherever it does not make it exist, so its drop does nothing, as the reference's does.
- _Two of the checker's re-rootings at the join disagreed with the reference_ (a place reached through a lent prefix was dropped at its parent; an ill-typed place in an arm the checker does not run was dropped at its parent, on an accepted program). Main changed the checker (D94): the place is dropped where it exists, and otherwise the definition is rejected.
- _The reference's drop read a holder through its content_, which reads `moved` where the content was moved out through the borrow (accepted, `Regress/HolderMoved.lean`), so it did nothing where the checker ends the borrow. Fixed: the reference tests the holder first, as the checker tests its stored value. _And three more facts about the checker's states were needed and are now clauses_, (WF14)–(WF16) above, with three more about its typing of places (a constructor value is of a data declaration; a variable holding a type is declared a sort; a proof below a variable is declared a copy, first stated at every place and false at a variable declared a borrow of a proposition); each was swept on every recorded state first.
- _The reference's drop read the place at a state no checker state is related to_, after the lent prefix's access and before its own, so proving it needed a lemma that an access changes no reading, with a side condition on the place's own borrow. It now reads the place after its own access, as every read of a place does; no result changes. _And a recorded η-refinement had to mean its unknown at every expected type_, which needs it to name its declaration's parameters: a clause of the checker's typing ((Hη), swept on 11,769 states holding one).
- _The statements for `Id` and `Eq` said nothing_, through a misplaced bracket, and footprint coverage lacked [Id-typestate]'s hypothesis; both restated.
- _Statements about single steps were false as first written_ and are restated, among them: [Access] at a borrow variable written whole ends nothing, where the statement had it end the variable's borrows; an assignment's statement missed the end of the old borrow; and a frame's pop drops temporaries without ending their borrows, so it needs the hypothesis that they hold none.

*What is open,* in the order it is being taken, is @sec-order.

